"use strict";

// All examples are educational; project prompts do not assert undocumented implementation details.
const GUIDE = {
  stages: ["Foundations", "Post-training", "Production systems", "Agentic systems"],
  lessons: [
    {
      id: "transformers", stage: 0, title: "Think in transformers", subtitle: "From tokens to next-token probabilities",
      time: 50, tags: ["architecture", "attention", "embeddings", "causal mask", "RoPE"], icon: "01",
      objective: "Explain the decoder block, track tensor shapes, and derive scaled dot-product attention without hand-waving.",
      sections: [
        { title: "Architecture first: what is a transformer?", body: `<p>A transformer is a stack of blocks that repeatedly updates a vector representation for each token. <strong>Attention mixes information across token positions</strong>; an <strong>MLP mixes features within each position</strong>. Residual connections carry the incoming hidden state around those transformations, and normalization helps control the scale of their inputs or outputs. The architecture specifies this computation; pretraining and fine-tuning specify how its parameters are learned.</p><p>The original Transformer was an encoder–decoder model for sequence-to-sequence tasks such as translation. Many modern generative LLMs use only a decoder-style stack. It is important to distinguish three families before studying an attention equation:</p><div class="viz-comparison-scroll"><table class="concept-table"><caption>Three transformer families</caption><thead><tr><th>Family</th><th>What can each position see?</th><th>Typical output / use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Encoder-only</td><td>Usually all non-padding positions in the input: bidirectional self-attention.</td><td>Contextual representations for classification, tagging, embeddings, or masked-token prediction.</td></tr><tr><td>Decoder-only</td><td>Current and earlier positions: causal self-attention.</td><td>Next-token probabilities for text generation; the prompt and answer form one sequence.</td></tr><tr><td>Encoder–decoder</td><td>Encoder sees the source bidirectionally. Decoder uses causal self-attention plus cross-attention to encoder states.</td><td>A target sequence conditioned on a separate source sequence, such as translation or summarization.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>In the architecture visual, switch between these families. For cross-attention, <strong>Q comes from decoder hidden states while K and V come from encoder outputs</strong>. Their query and source sequence lengths can differ. The square T×T attention matrix in the worked self-attention example is therefore not a universal shape.</p>` },
        { title: "Walk through one decoder block before the math", body: `<p>Begin with X, a hidden-state tensor [B,T,d_model]. In a common pre-normalized decoder block, apply a normalization to X, perform causal multi-head attention, project the concatenated head outputs, then add X back. Call the result H. Normalize H, pass each token through the same MLP, then add H back. Both residual additions require matching shapes.</p><div class="equation">H = X + MultiHeadAttention(Norm(X))<br>Y = H + MLP(Norm(H))</div><p><strong>Why attention?</strong> The representation of "it" can incorporate information from a preceding noun. <strong>Why the MLP?</strong> The collected information still needs nonlinear feature processing, not just another weighted average of tokens. A simple MLP expands width d to a larger intermediate width, applies a nonlinearity, and projects back; gated variants combine multiple projections. <strong>Why residuals?</strong> They provide an additive information/gradient path across layers. <strong>Why normalization?</strong> It stabilizes feature scales; the exact operation and placement are architecture choices.</p><p>LayerNorm centers and rescales features, while RMSNorm rescales by root mean square without the same mean-centering step. Pre-norm and post-norm place normalization differently. Do not copy the equations above into a claim that every transformer follows exactly this layout. Position handling, attention type, MLP gating, and normalization differ across checkpoints.</p>` },
        { title: "Trace every tensor from input to output", body: `<p>Take a toy batch B=2, length T=4, model width d=8, H=2 attention heads, and vocabulary size 6. Embedding lookup maps token IDs [2,4] to [2,4,8]. Each Q/K/V projection produces [2,4,8] for ordinary multi-head attention, then splitting heads and transposing gives [2,2,4,4]. The final 4 is d_head=8/2.</p><div class="equation">Token IDs [2,4]<br>&darr; embedding lookup<br>Hidden states [2,4,8]<br>&darr; Q/K/V projections and head split<br>Q, K, V [2,2,4,4]<br>&darr; Q @ K.transpose(-2,-1)<br>Scores [2,2,4,4] = [batch, head, query position, key position]<br>&darr; scale + mask + row softmax; then @ V<br>Head outputs [2,2,4,4]<br>&darr; transpose, concatenate, output projection<br>Hidden states [2,4,8]<br>&darr; repeat blocks, final norm, vocabulary projection<br>Logits [2,4,6]</div><p>Several dimensions happen to equal 4 in this example, but they have different meanings. Track axis names, not just numbers. Changing vocabulary size changes the output head, not the attention-score matrix. Changing sequence length changes query/key axes. Changing the number of heads redistributes feature dimensions in this ordinary MHA example; GQA/MQA use different KV-head counts.</p>` },
        { title: "Start with the prediction problem", body: `<p>A language model estimates p(x<sub>t</sub> | x<sub>&lt;t</sub>). A tokenizer maps text to integer IDs; an embedding lookup maps each ID to a learned vector. For a batch of B sequences with T tokens and model width d, the hidden state has shape [B, T, d]. The output projection produces [B, T, V] logits over vocabulary size V. Softmax gives a distribution, not a guarantee of truth.</p><p>Tokenization matters operationally: a rare name or date may split into many pieces, changing cost and generation behavior. Training predicts the next token at every valid position in parallel. Inference usually generates sequentially, conditioning on its previous outputs.</p>` },
        { title: "Give Q, K, and V a concrete meaning", body: `<p>For each token, a query vector is its learned matching request, a key vector is what other queries can match against, and a value vector supplies the content to mix into the result. These are learned continuous vectors—not literal keywords, database primary keys, or the user's search query. The same token has different Q, K, and V because the projection matrices have different parameters.</p><p>Suppose the query for "like" is [0,1] and the key for "I" is [1,0]. Their dot product is 0×1 + 1×0 = 0. A different key [1,1] gives a dot product of 1. This one scalar scores that query/key pair; it is not the final answer. Compute a score against every allowed key, scale and normalize those scores, then use the resulting weights to mix values. The Q/K/V visual highlights the row, column, scalar result, and weighted-value sum at each step.</p><p>Why transpose K? K is stored as one vector per token, [T,d_head]. A matrix product sums along a shared inner dimension. Q[T,d_head] @ K_transpose[d_head,T] produces [query T,key T]. It compares every query row with every key row without a Python loop. In batched code, transpose the last two axes; transposing the wrong axes can mix batch or head dimensions instead.</p>` },
        { title: "Derive one attention head", body: `<div class="equation">Q = XW<sub>Q</sub>, K = XW<sub>K</sub>, V = XW<sub>V</sub><br>Attention(Q,K,V) = softmax(QK<sup>T</sup> / &radic;d<sub>k</sub> + M)V</div><p>For one head, Q and K are [T, d<sub>k</sub>] and V is [T, d<sub>v</sub>]. QK<sup>T</sup> is [T, T]: one compatibility score per query/key pair. The softmax is over keys for each query. Scaling by &radic;d<sub>k</sub> prevents the dot-product variance from growing with head dimension and pushing softmax into saturation. The causal mask adds negative infinity to future positions before softmax, preventing label leakage.</p><p>Multiple heads learn different projections; their outputs are concatenated and projected back to width d. A typical decoder block combines attention, residual connections, normalization, and a position-wise MLP (often gated). Architectures differ in normalization placement, bias, and attention variants: do not claim every decoder is identical.</p>` },
        { title: "Position, context, and complexity", body: `<p>Attention alone is permutation equivariant, so order needs a signal. Absolute embeddings add position vectors; RoPE rotates query/key pairs so their dot products depend on relative offsets. RoPE is not a license to extrapolate arbitrarily beyond the training context. Long-context quality must be measured, including retrieval at different positions.</p><p>Dense attention has O(T²d) compute and a naive O(T²) attention-score footprint per head. The MLP and projections also cost compute; at short context they may dominate. During decoding, KV caching avoids recomputing old keys/values but consumes memory growing linearly with sequence length. Use the lab to distinguish the quadratic score matrix from the linear KV cache.</p>` },
        { title: "Work through a tiny example", body: `<p>Suppose a query has scores [2, 1, 0]. Subtract the maximum before exponentiating: [1, e<sup>-1</sup>, e<sup>-2</sup>]. Normalizing gives approximately [0.665, 0.245, 0.090]. A value vector is therefore a weighted mixture, not the top token's value alone. If the third position is masked, its weight becomes zero and the remaining weights renormalize to [0.731, 0.269].</p><p><strong>Implementation exercise:</strong> implement stable softmax, apply the mask before normalization, and compare a batched attention implementation against a loop on a small tensor. Test that changing a future token cannot affect an earlier position. Explain why attention weights are not a complete explanation of model decisions.</p>` },
        { title: "Practice explaining the trade-offs", body: `<p>Use a synthetic task or an example you are authorized to discuss. State the problem, baseline, data contracts, model choices, evaluation design, and failure modes. Separate assumptions from observations and do not invent impact numbers.</p><p>Design an ablation that isolates the proposed change. Define metric denominators, uncertainty, quality and latency gates, a staged rollout, and rollback conditions. Explain an alternative and what evidence would change your decision.</p>` }
      ],
      sources: [["Attention Is All You Need", "https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.03762"], ["RoFormer / rotary position embeddings", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.09864"]],
      questions: [
        ["Why divide attention logits by the square root of head dimension?", ["To reduce score variance growth and softmax saturation", "To eliminate the causal mask", "To compress the vocabulary", "To make attention linear in context"], 0, "Dot-product variance grows with dimension under common assumptions. Scaling stabilizes the softmax; it does not change quadratic attention complexity."],
        ["A causal mask must be applied at which point?", ["After multiplying attention weights by V", "To token IDs before embedding", "To scores before the softmax", "Only during inference"], 2, "Masking logits before softmax prevents future positions receiving probability mass. It is also needed in causal training."],
        ["With 12 heads and model width 768, what is the usual per-head width?", ["12", "64", "768", "9,216"], 1, "The usual split is d / h = 768 / 12 = 64. Architecture-specific variants can differ."]
      ]
    },
    {
      id: "training", stage: 0, title: "How language models learn", subtitle: "Losses, data, decoding, and reliable baselines",
      time: 25, tags: ["cross entropy", "perplexity", "sampling", "data leakage"], icon: "02",
      objective: "Trace a training step, distinguish perplexity from product quality, and reason about decoding trade-offs.",
      sections: [
        { title: "The next-token objective", body: `<div class="equation">L = - (1/N) &Sigma;<sub>valid tokens</sub> log p<sub>&theta;</sub>(x<sub>t</sub> | x<sub>&lt;t</sub>)</div><p>Shift inputs and targets by one token. Compute cross entropy from logits using a numerically stable log-softmax; ignore padding and any other deliberately masked targets. N counts valid supervised tokens, not padded positions. Backpropagation produces gradients; an optimizer such as AdamW updates parameters. Gradient accumulation simulates a larger effective batch but does not reduce the activation memory of a single microbatch.</p><p>Perplexity is exp(average negative log likelihood) with natural logs. Lower perplexity on a clean held-out corpus can indicate better modeling, but perplexities across different tokenizers or corpora are not directly comparable. A fluent wrong answer can have high likelihood.</p>` },
        { title: "Data quality is model quality", body: `<p>Deduplicate before splitting, remove contamination and sensitive data, and track provenance. Use temporal splits when future facts would leak into past evaluation. A random split of near-duplicate queries or documents can inflate performance. Inspect performance by query type, entity rarity, language, and answerability instead of relying on one global average.</p><p>For an election/date answering system, label the time at which an answer was valid. Distinguish stale evidence from reasoning errors. Test boundary dates and ambiguous entities. Preserve an untouched evaluation set while iterating on training data and prompts.</p>` },
        { title: "Choose a decoding policy deliberately", body: `<p>Greedy decoding chooses the highest-probability next token. Temperature rescales logits: lower values sharpen the distribution; higher values flatten it. Top-p sampling retains a minimal high-probability set whose cumulative mass reaches p. Top-k caps the candidate count. These change generation behavior, not underlying model knowledge.</p><p>For factual short answers, start with a low-variance decoding policy and explicit output constraints. For brainstorming, diversity may help. Fix seeds where supported, but do not assume complete GPU determinism. Compare policies on factuality, format validity, response length, and latency.</p>` },
        { title: "Diagnose before spending more compute", body: `<p>If training loss falls but validation loss rises, investigate overfitting, distribution shift, and leakage in preprocessing. If both remain high, inspect target alignment, optimizer settings, data quality, model capacity, and token masks. Log gradient norms and learning rate. A loss spike alone does not identify a root cause.</p><p><strong>Hands-on drill:</strong> write pseudocode for a training step with zero_grad, forward, masked loss, backward, clipping, and step. Explain where mixed-precision scaling belongs and why clipping occurs after unscaling. Overfit one tiny batch as a wiring check, not as evidence of generalization.</p>` },
        { title: "Practice explaining the trade-offs", body: `<p>Use a synthetic task or an example you are authorized to discuss. State the problem, baseline, data contracts, model choices, evaluation design, and failure modes. Separate assumptions from observations and do not invent impact numbers.</p><p>Design an ablation that isolates the proposed change. Define metric denominators, uncertainty, quality and latency gates, a staged rollout, and rollback conditions. Explain an alternative and what evidence would change your decision.</p>` }
      ],
      sources: [["The Illustrated Transformer", "https://jalammar.github.io/illustrated-transformer/"], ["Hugging Face generation strategies", "https://huggingface.co/docs/transformers/generation_strategies"]],
      questions: [
        ["Perplexity decreases. What can you conclude?", ["Human satisfaction must increase", "The model assigns greater average likelihood on that evaluation setup", "The model no longer hallucinates", "Different tokenizers are now comparable"], 1, "Perplexity measures held-out likelihood under a specific corpus and tokenization, not guaranteed factuality or user satisfaction."],
        ["Near-duplicate documents exist across train and test. What is the primary risk?", ["Lower inference throughput", "An oversized vocabulary", "Evaluation contamination inflates generalization estimates", "Too many attention heads"], 2, "Deduplicate or group related examples before splitting to prevent memorization being mistaken for generalization."],
        ["What does higher temperature generally do?", ["Flattens the next-token distribution", "Adds new facts to the model", "Reduces parameter count", "Guarantees longer answers"], 0, "Temperature divides logits before softmax. Higher values usually increase sampling diversity, but do not guarantee any response length."]
      ]
    },
    {
      id: "sft", stage: 1, title: "Supervised fine-tuning, done right", subtitle: "Instruction data, response masking, and LoRA",
      time: 35, tags: ["SFT", "LoRA", "QLoRA", "chat templates"], icon: "03",
      objective: "Design a reproducible SFT experiment and explain precisely what parameter-efficient adaptation saves.",
      sections: [
        { title: "Teach a behavior, not a database", body: `<p>SFT minimizes cross entropy on demonstrations of desired behavior. It is well suited to task format, style, domain vocabulary, tool-call syntax, and evidence-grounded response habits. It is not a reliable store for frequently changing factual knowledge; fresh information usually belongs in retrieval or tools.</p><p>Build examples that represent the production input: instruction, evidence, optional conversation, and target answer. Include unanswerable and conflicting-evidence examples so the model learns when to abstain. Review labels and keep data provenance. More noisy demonstrations can be worse than fewer well-curated ones.</p>` },
        { title: "Get the training surface right", body: `<p>Use the checkpoint's chat template and special tokens consistently in training and serving. If training only assistant responses, mask user/system/padding labels with the framework's ignore index. Other objectives may intentionally supervise more tokens; state the choice. Never accidentally train the model to reproduce padded targets.</p><p>Packing improves utilization but needs correct sequence boundaries and attention handling. Truncation must not remove the answer or create misleading evidence. Check actual tokenized examples, EOS placement, loss masks, and multi-turn tool messages before launching a large job.</p>` },
        { title: "LoRA and QLoRA: what changes?", body: `<div class="equation">W' = W + (&alpha;/r)BA<br>W: [d<sub>out</sub>, d<sub>in</sub>], B: [d<sub>out</sub>, r], A: [r, d<sub>in</sub>]</div><p>LoRA freezes a base weight W and trains low-rank updates. Instead of d<sub>out</sub>d<sub>in</sub> trainable values, it adds r(d<sub>in</sub>+d<sub>out</sub>). For a 4096 by 4096 matrix with r=16, that is 131,072 adapter parameters versus 16,777,216 original weights. Target modules and rank influence capacity; choose them by evaluation, not folklore.</p><p>QLoRA keeps a quantized frozen base and trains adapters, with details such as NF4 in the original work. It saves base-weight and optimizer memory relative to full fine-tuning, but activations and other state still matter. Quantized-base adapter training is not the same as arbitrary end-to-end 4-bit training. Merging and deployment compatibility require checks.</p>` },
        { title: "A defensible experiment matrix", body: `<p>Start with base model + fixed retrieval. Compare SFT at a few learning rates and adapter ranks on a development split. Record checkpoint, dataset version, prompt template, max length, precision, seed, optimizer, effective batch, supervised-token count, and compute. Watch task quality, general ability retention, abstention, and output validity, not loss alone.</p><p>Run a final untouched test once the choice is fixed. Include a latency and memory comparison under identical request distributions. Fine-tuning may improve quality but does not automatically make a model smaller or faster. Keep artifacts and a rollback path.</p>` },
        { title: "Practice explaining the trade-offs", body: `<p>Use a synthetic task or an example you are authorized to discuss. State the problem, baseline, data contracts, model choices, evaluation design, and failure modes. Separate assumptions from observations and do not invent impact numbers.</p><p>Design an ablation that isolates the proposed change. Define metric denominators, uncertainty, quality and latency gates, a staged rollout, and rollback conditions. Explain an alternative and what evidence would change your decision.</p>` }
      ],
      sources: [["LoRA", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.09685"], ["QLoRA", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.14314"], ["TRL SFT trainer", "https://huggingface.co/docs/trl/sft_trainer"]],
      questions: [
        ["For a 4096 x 4096 weight with LoRA rank 16, how many adapter parameters are added (without biases)?", ["16,777,216", "4,096", "65,536", "131,072"], 3, "r(d_in + d_out) = 16(4096 + 4096) = 131,072."],
        ["Why keep the chat template consistent between SFT and serving?", ["It eliminates the need for data splitting", "It avoids a formatting and special-token distribution mismatch", "It guarantees zero hallucinations", "It removes all padding"], 1, "A mismatch in role separators or special tokens can change the input distribution and undermine learned behavior."],
        ["Which information is usually better provided by retrieval than memorized through SFT?", ["A stable response schema", "The desired tone", "Rapidly changing factual records", "How to emit a tool call"], 2, "Retrieval can refresh facts without retraining. SFT is better for behavior and task adaptation."]
      ]
    },
    {
      id: "dpo", stage: 1, title: "Learn from preferences with DPO", subtitle: "Pairwise data, reference policies, and pitfalls",
      time: 30, tags: ["DPO", "preferences", "reference policy", "beta"], icon: "04",
      objective: "Derive the DPO margin and explain why a preference loss is not a direct factuality metric.",
      sections: [
        { title: "From demonstrations to comparisons", body: `<p>Preference data contains a prompt x, chosen response y<sub>w</sub>, and rejected response y<sub>l</sub>. Both responses must answer the same prompt and context. Annotators need a rubric: correctness, grounding, relevance, safety, and concision may conflict. Preserve ties or ambiguity in the data process rather than forcing unreliable labels.</p><p>DPO optimizes a policy directly from these comparisons using a fixed reference policy, commonly an SFT model. In the standard formulation, it does not train a separate reward model or collect on-policy rollouts inside the loss loop. That simpler pipeline still needs careful data and validation.</p>` },
        { title: "Read the objective term by term", body: `<div class="equation">m = log [&pi;<sub>&theta;</sub>(y<sub>w</sub>|x) / &pi;<sub>ref</sub>(y<sub>w</sub>|x)]<br>&nbsp;&nbsp; - log [&pi;<sub>&theta;</sub>(y<sub>l</sub>|x) / &pi;<sub>ref</sub>(y<sub>l</sub>|x)]<br>L<sub>DPO</sub> = -log &sigma;(&beta;m)</div><p>Response sequence log probabilities are sums over response tokens with correct masking. The loss favors a greater relative log-probability margin for the chosen answer compared with the reference. If m=0, the loss is log 2, approximately 0.693. A positive margin decreases the loss.</p><p>In the underlying KL-regularized derivation, beta controls the reference constraint; larger beta corresponds to stronger regularization in that objective. In the pairwise loss it also rescales the margin, so training dynamics depend on implementation, data, and hyperparameters. Do not reduce beta to an unconditional claim that a larger value always improves or worsens quality.</p>` },
        { title: "What preference training can get wrong", body: `<p>A judge may prefer longer, confident, or stylistically polished answers even when they are false. Control response-length artifacts and evaluate correctness separately. Noisy preference labels, off-distribution pairs, and over-optimization can harm general ability. Standard sequence log-probability sums have length-related effects; understand any normalized objective used by your library rather than silently changing the formula.</p><p>Hold out prompts, not just individual responses. Test paired win rates with randomized answer ordering, blinded labels, and uncertainty estimates. Track abstention and unsupported-claim rate alongside preference wins. Compare against both base and SFT checkpoints.</p>` },
        { title: "A worked search-answer pair", body: `<p>Imagine the evidence says a date is not yet announced. Response A clearly says so and cites the evidence; response B invents a date in a polished sentence. The rubric should prefer A for grounded correctness. If your preference source favors B, optimizing the preference loss can make the product worse.</p><p><strong>Exercise:</strong> let the chosen relative log-probability be 0.4 and rejected be -0.2. The margin is 0.6. At beta=0.1, the scaled margin is 0.06 and loss is approximately 0.664. Show how a mislabeled pair pushes in the wrong direction.</p>` },
        { title: "Practice explaining the trade-offs", body: `<p>Use a synthetic task or an example you are authorized to discuss. State the problem, baseline, data contracts, model choices, evaluation design, and failure modes. Separate assumptions from observations and do not invent impact numbers.</p><p>Design an ablation that isolates the proposed change. Define metric denominators, uncertainty, quality and latency gates, a staged rollout, and rollback conditions. Explain an alternative and what evidence would change your decision.</p>` }
      ],
      sources: [["Direct Preference Optimization", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.18290"], ["TRL DPO trainer", "https://huggingface.co/docs/trl/dpo_trainer"]],
      questions: [
        ["What is required by standard DPO training?", ["An online environment at every update", "A separately trained reward model", "Chosen/rejected responses and a reference policy", "Only unlabeled raw documents"], 2, "Standard DPO uses preference pairs and a reference policy; it avoids a separately trained reward model and on-policy sampling loop."],
        ["If the DPO relative margin is zero, what is the per-pair loss?", ["0", "Approximately 0.693", "1.693", "Negative infinity"], 1, "-log sigmoid(0) = -log(0.5) = log(2)."],
        ["Preference win rate improves but unsupported claims increase. What should you do?", ["Ship because the loss decreased", "Only increase answer length", "Remove the held-out set", "Inspect the rubric and labels and gate on grounded correctness"], 3, "Preferences can reward style over truth. Grounding and factuality need independent validation and release gates."]
      ]
    },
    {
      id: "ppo", stage: 1, title: "Understand RLHF and PPO", subtitle: "Rewards, advantages, clipping, and KL",
      time: 35, tags: ["PPO", "RLHF", "advantages", "reward hacking"], icon: "05",
      objective: "Explain an RLHF rollout/update cycle and distinguish PPO clipping from reference-policy regularization.",
      sections: [
        { title: "The classical RLHF pipeline", body: `<p>A common pipeline starts with SFT, trains a reward model from ranked responses, then optimizes the policy on generated responses. The reward model often uses a pairwise logistic loss on scalar scores. Its score is a learned proxy for human preference, not objective truth.</p><p>For language generation, the prompt and previous generated tokens form a state, the next token is an action, and a completed response receives reward. A value function estimates expected returns. Rollout cost, multiple model copies, and reward reliability make this substantially more operationally complex than offline preference training.</p>` },
        { title: "Read the clipped policy objective", body: `<div class="equation">r<sub>t</sub>(&theta;) = &pi;<sub>&theta;</sub>(a<sub>t</sub>|s<sub>t</sub>) / &pi;<sub>old</sub>(a<sub>t</sub>|s<sub>t</sub>)<br>L<sup>clip</sup> = E[min(r<sub>t</sub>A&#770;<sub>t</sub>, clip(r<sub>t</sub>,1-&epsilon;,1+&epsilon;)A&#770;<sub>t</sub>)]</div><p>This surrogate is maximized. Advantages measure whether an action performed better than expected; in practice they may be estimated with generalized advantage estimation. Clipping limits incentives for certain overly large probability-ratio changes over repeated minibatch updates. It is not a hard bound on the final KL divergence and does not guarantee safe behavior.</p><p>If A=2, r=1.3, and epsilon=0.2, the terms are 2.6 and 2.4, so the minimum is 2.4. For negative advantages the minimum still matters; do not apply the positive-advantage intuition blindly. Value loss and entropy terms may also be used.</p>` },
        { title: "Two policies, two different constraints", body: `<p>The old rollout policy is a recent snapshot used for the importance ratio. The reference policy is usually a fixed starting checkpoint used to penalize drift. These have different roles. A reward may include -beta times a token-level log-ratio to the reference, which estimates a KL penalty in expectation under appropriate sampling. Clipping relative to the old policy does not replace reference regularization.</p><p>Track approximate KL, reward distributions, response lengths, entropy, value error, clipping fraction, and held-out task quality. A growing reward with falling human-rated quality is a warning of reward hacking or proxy misspecification.</p>` },
        { title: "Choose DPO or PPO for a reason", body: `<p>Prefer a simpler offline method when you have reliable static preference pairs and limited rollout infrastructure. Consider on-policy RL when you can collect useful feedback on newly generated behavior, especially when verifiable task rewards or interactions matter. Neither method automatically fixes missing knowledge or a bad reward.</p><p>For a tool agent, a "successful" outcome needs checks for correctness, resource use, and unauthorized side effects. Sparse terminal rewards complicate credit assignment. Unit-testable coding tasks have different reward properties from subjective search answers.</p>` },
        { title: "Practice explaining the trade-offs", body: `<p>Use a synthetic task or an example you are authorized to discuss. State the problem, baseline, data contracts, model choices, evaluation design, and failure modes. Separate assumptions from observations and do not invent impact numbers.</p><p>Design an ablation that isolates the proposed change. Define metric denominators, uncertainty, quality and latency gates, a staged rollout, and rollback conditions. Explain an alternative and what evidence would change your decision.</p>` }
      ],
      sources: [["Proximal Policy Optimization", "https://arxiv.org/abs/1707.06347"], ["Training language models to follow instructions", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.02155"]],
      questions: [
        ["Which policies appear in PPO's importance ratio?", ["Current policy and fixed reference only", "Current policy and old rollout policy", "Reward model and retriever", "Two randomly selected checkpoints"], 1, "The ratio compares current action probabilities with those used to collect rollouts. Reference KL is a separate constraint."],
        ["For A=2, r=1.3 and epsilon=0.2, what is the clipped surrogate term?", ["2.6", "0.2", "2.4", "1.2"], 2, "min(1.3*2, 1.2*2) = min(2.6, 2.4) = 2.4."],
        ["Reward rises while factual quality falls. What is a plausible explanation?", ["Guaranteed convergence to truth", "The tokenizer has become lossless", "KV caching is disabled", "The policy is exploiting a flawed reward proxy"], 3, "Reward hacking occurs when the policy exploits proxy weaknesses. Investigate reward validity and held-out behavior."]
      ]
    },
    {
      id: "inference", stage: 2, title: "Make inference fast, not fragile", subtitle: "KV cache, batching, quantization, and TensorRT",
      time: 35, tags: ["KV cache", "TensorRT", "quantization", "FlashAttention"], icon: "06",
      objective: "Build a latency budget, calculate cache memory, and benchmark optimizations without hiding quality regressions.",
      sections: [
        { title: "Separate prefill from decode", body: `<p>Prefill processes prompt tokens and creates the KV cache. Decode produces new tokens, commonly one step at a time per active sequence. Time to first token (TTFT), inter-token latency, and end-to-end latency reveal different bottlenecks. Queueing, retrieval, network calls, and serialization can dominate the model itself.</p><p>Report p50/p95/p99 as appropriate, input/output token distributions, concurrency, and hardware. Throughput under saturation is not interactive latency under normal load. Longer generated answers can raise end-to-end latency even when tokens per second improves.</p>` },
        { title: "Calculate the cache", body: `<div class="equation">KV bytes = 2 &times; B &times; T &times; L &times; H<sub>KV</sub> &times; d<sub>head</sub> &times; bytes/value</div><p>The factor 2 counts keys and values. With batch=1, context=4096, layers=32, KV heads=8, head width=128 and BF16 (2 bytes), the idealized cache is 536,870,912 bytes, or 512 MiB. This excludes weights, activations, workspaces, fragmentation, and allocator overhead. GQA reduces KV heads relative to query heads; MQA uses one KV head.</p><p>Paged cache management can reduce allocation waste; continuous batching admits and retires requests between decode steps. Neither removes compute cost. Cache reuse needs exact prefix and configuration compatibility plus strict tenant isolation.</p>` },
        { title: "Choose the right optimization", body: `<p>FlashAttention uses tiled, IO-aware computation to avoid materializing the full attention matrix in high-bandwidth memory. It computes exact attention up to numerical precision; it does not generally make dense attention's arithmetic complexity linear. Quantization reduces precision and can reduce memory or accelerate supported kernels, but speed depends on hardware, shapes, and overhead.</p><p>TensorRT-family engines can optimize graphs and kernels for supported models, precision modes, and shape profiles. Validate conversion and tokenizer parity. Speculative decoding uses a draft model and acceptance procedure; speed depends on acceptance rate and relative costs, not the label "speculative" alone.</p>` },
        { title: "FlashAttention from the algorithm to the kernel", body: `<p>Naive attention writes a T×T score/probability matrix to high-bandwidth device memory and rereads it. FlashAttention tiles Q/K/V into faster on-chip memory and accumulates the output without materializing that entire matrix. It uses an online softmax that maintains a running maximum and normalization sum across key blocks. Updating a maximum requires rescaling earlier partial sums; simply normalizing each tile independently would be wrong.</p><p>For blocks with running maximum m and sum l, a new block produces m' = max(m, max(scores)) and l' = exp(m−m')l + Σ exp(scores−m'). The output numerator is rescaled consistently before adding the new weighted values. Backward recomputes selected intermediates instead of storing the full matrix, trading arithmetic for reduced memory traffic. Numerical differences from accumulation order are expected; validate tolerances rather than bitwise equality.</p><p>FlashAttention-2 improves parallelism and work partitioning to reduce non-matrix-multiply work and shared-memory communication. Newer kernels are hardware- and software-specific; do not assume one version fits every GPU, dtype, mask, head dimension, or layout. FlashAttention primarily targets attention IO, while paged KV caching targets serving allocation; these solve different problems and may coexist.</p><p>In PyTorch, scaled_dot_product_attention may select a fused or math backend based on constraints. Calling that function does not prove a FlashAttention kernel ran. Inspect backend diagnostics or a profiler trace. Measure prefill and decode separately; single-query decode can be limited by KV reads and may benefit differently than long-sequence training.</p>` },
        { title: "A trustworthy benchmark plan", body: `<p>Warm up, include representative variable-length requests, and measure at fixed load before testing the capacity curve. Record OOMs, timeouts, error rates, and quality. Test short/long prompts and outputs separately. Compare the same hardware and request mix; keep a rollback checkpoint and engine configuration.</p><p>If latency drops from 100 ms to 70 ms, the reduction is 30%. That does not imply a 30% throughput gain. QPS depends on batching, concurrency, resource utilization, and SLOs. Specify whether the timing covers model execution or the full request.</p>` },
        { title: "Practice explaining the trade-offs", body: `<p>Use a synthetic task or an example you are authorized to discuss. State the problem, baseline, data contracts, model choices, evaluation design, and failure modes. Separate assumptions from observations and do not invent impact numbers.</p><p>Design an ablation that isolates the proposed change. Define metric denominators, uncertainty, quality and latency gates, a staged rollout, and rollback conditions. Explain an alternative and what evidence would change your decision.</p>` }
      ],
      sources: [["FlashAttention", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.14135"], ["FlashAttention-2", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.08691"], ["PyTorch scaled dot-product attention", "https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/stable/generated/torch.nn.functional.scaled_dot_product_attention.html"], ["PagedAttention / vLLM", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.06180"], ["TensorRT-LLM documentation", "https://nvidia.github.io/TensorRT-LLM/"]],
      questions: [
        ["What is the ideal BF16 KV cache for B=1, T=4096, L=32, H_KV=8, d_head=128?", ["32 MiB", "512 MiB", "4 MiB", "16 GiB"], 1, "2*1*4096*32*8*128*2 = 536,870,912 bytes = 512 MiB, excluding overhead and weights."],
        ["What does FlashAttention primarily improve?", ["The IO and materialization cost of exact attention", "It removes all quadratic arithmetic", "It eliminates the need for keys", "It guarantees factual answers"], 0, "Tiling and IO awareness avoid storing the full score matrix in high-bandwidth memory while preserving exact attention within numerical precision."],
        ["What is needed to interpret a claim of 30% lower latency?", ["Only the model name", "Only the token vocabulary size", "Hardware, workload, load, percentile, timing scope, and quality parity", "A single fastest request"], 2, "Latency is workload- and load-dependent. A representative benchmark and quality comparison make the claim meaningful."]
      ]
    },
    {
      id: "retrieval", stage: 2, title: "Retrieve, rerank, and know the difference", subtitle: "Dense search, hybrid retrieval, and ranking metrics",
      time: 35, tags: ["dense retrieval", "BM25", "reranking", "NDCG"], icon: "07",
      objective: "Design a multi-stage search stack and diagnose whether relevance failures come from candidate generation or ordering.",
      sections: [
        { title: "Candidate generation is a recall problem", body: `<p>A bi-encoder embeds query and documents independently, making precomputed document vectors searchable. Contrastive objectives encourage relevant pairs to score above negatives. Hard negatives improve discrimination but false negatives can penalize genuinely relevant documents. Inspect sampling and labels.</p><p>BM25 uses lexical matching and is strong for exact terms, identifiers, and rare names. Dense retrieval helps semantic matching. Hybrid retrieval combines complementary candidate sets; reciprocal rank fusion uses rank positions rather than assuming raw scores from different systems are calibrated. ANN index recall is distinct from relevance recall: an approximate index may miss the exact vector neighbors, and even exact vector neighbors may not be relevant.</p>` },
        { title: "Spend compute on fewer candidates", body: `<p>A cross-encoder jointly encodes a query/document pair and can model rich interactions, but is usually too expensive across a huge corpus. Late-interaction multi-vector models encode token-level vectors and aggregate interactions, such as a MaxSim-style score. They trade index size and scoring cost for richer matching than one vector.</p><p>A practical funnel retrieves hundreds of candidates, reranks a smaller subset, then selects evidence or triggers suppression. Exact sizes depend on latency and quality curves. Always measure candidate recall before blaming the reranker: no reranker can recover a relevant document absent from its candidate set.</p>` },
        { title: "Compute ranking metrics", body: `<div class="equation">DCG@k = &Sigma;<sub>i=1..k</sub> (2<sup>rel<sub>i</sub></sup>-1) / log<sub>2</sub>(i+1)<br>NDCG@k = DCG@k / IDCG@k</div><p>With relevance [0,2,1], DCG@3 = 0 + 3/log2(3) + 1/log2(4), approximately 2.393. The ideal [2,1,0] has DCG approximately 3.631, so NDCG is about 0.659. State the relevance scale and gain convention. Decide and document how to handle queries with zero ideal gain.</p><p>Recall@k measures recovery of labeled relevant items; MRR emphasizes the first relevant result; NDCG supports graded relevance and position discounting. Incomplete judgments can bias all of these. Report uncertainty at the query level and inspect slices.</p>` },
        { title: "Indexes, query transforms, and evidence selection", body: `<p>HNSW trades graph memory and search effort for approximate-neighbor recall; IVF partitions vector space and searches selected cells; product quantization compresses vectors with an accuracy trade-off. Tune the index using exact-search neighbors as an ANN reference, then separately measure task relevance. Embedding model changes usually require a compatible re-embedding/index migration, not merely swapping the query encoder.</p><p>Query rewriting can resolve conversational references, while multi-query retrieval broadens candidate coverage. HyDE uses a hypothetical generated document as a retrieval representation; its generated facts are not evidence. These techniques can add latency and drift intent. Compare them on held-out original queries and retain the original query for exact names and constraints.</p><p>Context selection can use deduplication and maximal marginal relevance to balance relevance with diversity. More diversity is not always better: factual claims still need enough mutually consistent evidence. Document and evaluate the final evidence selection after reranking, not just the top-k retriever output.</p>` },
        { title: "Labels, freshness, and suppression", body: `<p>Use query-grouped and time-aware splits where appropriate. Guard against duplicate documents, popularity shortcuts, and mislabeled negatives. In volatile domains, indexing lag and stale documents can dominate model quality. Suppression is a decision threshold with precision/coverage trade-offs, not merely a model accuracy number.</p><p><strong>Exercise:</strong> compare lexical-only, dense-only, hybrid, reranking, and suppression ablations under a fixed latency budget. Plot quality versus candidate count. Inspect exact-match entities and long-tail queries separately.</p>` },
        { title: "Practice explaining the trade-offs", body: `<p>Use a synthetic task or an example you are authorized to discuss. State the problem, baseline, data contracts, model choices, evaluation design, and failure modes. Separate assumptions from observations and do not invent impact numbers.</p><p>Design an ablation that isolates the proposed change. Define metric denominators, uncertainty, quality and latency gates, a staged rollout, and rollback conditions. Explain an alternative and what evidence would change your decision.</p>` }
      ],
      sources: [["Dense Passage Retrieval", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.04906"], ["ColBERT", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.12832"], ["Information Retrieval evaluation", "https://nlp.stanford.edu/IR-book/html/htmledition/evaluation-in-information-retrieval-1.html"]],
      questions: [
        ["The relevant document never reaches the reranker. What should you investigate first?", ["Candidate generation recall", "Only the output font", "Only generation temperature", "The final answer length"], 0, "A reranker can reorder only its input. Fix candidate coverage or upstream indexing before expecting ranking to recover missing evidence."],
        ["Why use reciprocal rank fusion for hybrid search?", ["It retrains the embedding model", "It combines rankings without requiring calibrated raw scores", "It guarantees perfect recall", "It removes all lexical results"], 1, "Rank-based fusion avoids directly adding incomparable lexical and vector scores."],
        ["What distinguishes NDCG from an ungraded hit metric?", ["It measures GPU memory", "It ignores position", "It incorporates graded relevance and rank discounting", "It measures answer fluency only"], 2, "NDCG normalizes a position-discounted graded gain by the ideal ordering."]
      ]
    },
    {
      id: "rag", stage: 2, title: "Build RAG that earns its citations", subtitle: "Ingestion, grounding, abstention, and freshness",
      time: 35, tags: ["RAG", "chunking", "citations", "document updates"], icon: "08",
      objective: "Design a grounded RAG pipeline with permission-aware retrieval and component-level evaluation.",
      sections: [
        { title: "A complete pipeline, not a vector database", body: `<p>RAG includes ingestion, parsing, chunking, metadata, embedding, indexing, retrieval, reranking, context selection, generation, and evaluation. Store document IDs, versions, timestamps, section paths, access controls, and source spans. Deletions and access changes must propagate to indexes and caches.</p><p>Chunk by meaningful structure where possible. Small chunks improve targeting but lose context; large chunks include distractors and cost more tokens. Overlap helps continuity but creates duplication. Preserve table headers and section context. Evaluate chunking against actual queries rather than choosing a universal size.</p>` },
        { title: "Ground each claim in the right evidence", body: `<p>Build a prompt that distinguishes instructions from untrusted retrieved content. Ask for claims tied to source IDs and preserve source mappings through preprocessing. A syntactically valid citation can still fail to support its claim; assess citation precision, evidence completeness, and faithfulness separately.</p><p>When evidence is missing, stale, or contradictory, the system should abstain or ask for clarification according to product policy. Longer context does not guarantee better reasoning: duplicate or conflicting passages can degrade answers. Rerank and budget evidence rather than filling every available token.</p>` },
        { title: "Evaluate each failure boundary", body: `<p>First ask whether a relevant source exists in the authorized corpus. Then measure retrieval recall, reranking quality, context coverage, claim-level grounding, answer correctness, and useful abstention. Use an oracle-evidence experiment: if generation fails with perfect evidence, focus on prompting or model behavior; if it succeeds, upstream retrieval is implicated.</p><p>Maintain a test set with answerable, unanswerable, conflicting, stale, and access-restricted cases. Automated judges need a rubric and human calibration. An answer can be faithful to a stale document yet factually wrong today, so temporal correctness is an independent axis.</p>` },
        { title: "Document updates need stronger guarantees", body: `<p>For an automated documentation service, generate a proposed patch rather than silently overwriting the authoritative document. Attach source versions, a diff, provenance, and validation status. Require review for high-impact changes. Make writes idempotent and detect concurrent edits using version checks.</p><p>For sensitive documents, permission filters must run in the data-access path, not just in the language prompt. Do not leak unauthorized text into a prompt, cache, trace, or judge. Retrieval content is data, never authority to grant additional tool permissions.</p>` },
        { title: "An evaluation dataset with a real contract", body: `<p>Each test case needs a stable ID, query, user/tenant scope, as-of time, source snapshot, answerability label, relevant evidence IDs/spans, required claims or accepted answers, forbidden claims, and slice tags. Track who labeled it, agreement, and adjudication. Keep source versions fixed for reproducible offline evaluation and maintain a separate freshness suite for changing data.</p><div class="code-block">case_id: rag-017<br>query: "When does policy P become effective?"<br>scope: tenant-A / reviewer<br>as_of: 2026-01-15<br>source_snapshot: policy-v3<br>answerable: true<br>relevant_chunks: [P-v3-section-2]<br>required_claims: [effective_date = 2026-02-01]<br>forbidden_claims: [effective_date = 2025-02-01]<br>slices: [date, updated-document]</div><p>This is a synthetic fixture, not a fact about your work. Add paired cases for another tenant with no access, a pre-update snapshot, a missing source, and conflicting versions. Split by source family or entity when needed to prevent near-duplicate leakage; keep prompt tuning off the final test set.</p>` },
        { title: "The RAG scorecard: compute, don't just name", body: `<p><strong>Evidence recall@k:</strong> relevant gold evidence items retrieved / all gold relevant items. <strong>Evidence precision@k:</strong> relevant retrieved items / k, with duplicate and multi-span rules fixed. <strong>Context recall:</strong> required gold facts recoverable from the final supplied context / required gold facts. These depend on complete, consistent annotations.</p><p><strong>Claim support (faithfulness):</strong> supported generated factual claims / generated factual claims. <strong>Citation precision:</strong> supporting citation-claim links / all citation-claim links. <strong>Citation completeness:</strong> externally verifiable generated claims with supporting citations / all externally verifiable generated claims. Define treatment of empty answers explicitly; an empty answer should not become a perfect factual response through a zero denominator.</p><p><strong>Answer correctness:</strong> use exact match or token F1 only when appropriate, otherwise an adjudicated semantic rubric with required and forbidden claims. <strong>Abstention:</strong> measure correct abstention on unanswerable cases and unnecessary abstention on answerable cases. Report accuracy conditional on answering along with coverage over all requests and overall task utility.</p><div class="callout"><strong>Worked example</strong><p>Gold evidence is {A, B}; retrieved top-3 is {A, C, D}. Evidence recall is 1/2 and precision is 1/3. If an answer makes four factual claims and only three are supported, claim support is 3/4. These metrics diagnose different failure boundaries; averaging them into one unexplained "RAG score" loses that distinction.</p></div>` },
        { title: "Run the experiment end to end", body: `<p>Freeze the source snapshot, embedding/index version, query transform, reranker, prompt, generator, decoding, and judge version. Run a lexical baseline, dense baseline, hybrid/reranked system, and an oracle-evidence generator. Save retrieved IDs/scores, selected context, answers, claim labels, token use, timing, and errors for every case. Compare variants on the same cases with query-level uncertainty and slice analysis.</p><p>Calibrate any automated judge on human-labeled examples with known support and contradiction. Check evidence truncation, answer-order bias, and resistance to instructions embedded in candidate text. Deterministic checks can validate dates, required JSON fields, source IDs, and unauthorized citations; humans remain necessary for ambiguous entailment and usefulness.</p><p>Before launch, choose explicit acceptance criteria tied to the product: for example, no access violations in the security suite, a predeclared grounding threshold, no material regression on hard slices, and latency/error-rate SLOs. Threshold values are decisions to justify, not universal constants. Roll out gradually and monitor answer coverage, source age, index lag, support, complaints, p95 latency, token cost, and drift. Investigate logged failures with data minimization and retention controls.</p>` },
        { title: "Practice explaining the trade-offs", body: `<p>Use a synthetic task or an example you are authorized to discuss. State the problem, baseline, data contracts, model choices, evaluation design, and failure modes. Separate assumptions from observations and do not invent impact numbers.</p><p>Design an ablation that isolates the proposed change. Define metric denominators, uncertainty, quality and latency gates, a staged rollout, and rollback conditions. Explain an alternative and what evidence would change your decision.</p>` }
      ],
      sources: [["Retrieval-Augmented Generation", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.11401"], ["Lost in the Middle", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.03172"]],
      questions: [
        ["A citation link exists, but its source does not support the sentence. What failed?", ["Only tokenization", "Citation support / grounding", "GPU memory allocation", "The optimizer state"], 1, "Citation presence is not citation correctness. Verify whether the cited evidence entails the associated claim."],
        ["Why run an oracle-evidence experiment?", ["To replace all production evaluation", "To avoid permission checks", "To separate generation failures from upstream retrieval failures", "To increase context indefinitely"], 2, "Giving the generator ideal evidence helps isolate whether the bottleneck is retrieval or generation."],
        ["Where should document authorization be enforced?", ["Only in the prompt", "Only after the answer is displayed", "It is unnecessary for embeddings", "In the data-access path, including retrieval and cache handling"], 3, "Unauthorized data must never reach prompts, caches, traces, or downstream judges."]
      ]
    },
    {
      id: "evaluation", stage: 2, title: "Prove the improvement", subtitle: "Experiments, LLM judges, and the ML fundamentals",
      time: 35, tags: ["A/B testing", "F1", "confidence intervals", "LLM judge"], icon: "09",
      objective: "Choose appropriate metrics, avoid causal overclaims, and connect offline science with production outcomes.",
      sections: [
        { title: "Start with the decision and the unit", body: `<p>Define a primary outcome, guardrails, population, randomization unit, and minimum meaningful effect before running an A/B test. Users, sessions, or requests produce different dependence structures. Avoid treating correlated observations as independent. Check sample-ratio mismatch, logging parity, exposure, and interference.</p><p>Power depends on baseline variance, desired detectable effect, significance level, and sample size. Repeated peeking with ordinary fixed-horizon tests inflates false positives. Use a prespecified horizon or an appropriate sequential procedure. Report effect size and uncertainty, not just "significant."</p>` },
        { title: "Know your classification basics", body: `<div class="equation">Precision = TP/(TP+FP) &nbsp; Recall = TP/(TP+FN)<br>F1 = 2PR/(P+R)</div><p>For TP=80, FP=20, FN=40, precision is 0.8, recall is 0.667, and F1 is about 0.727. A threshold change shifts the precision/recall trade-off without changing the model weights. For imbalanced labels, inspect PR curves, slice errors, and business costs; high accuracy can be a majority-class illusion.</p><p>Calibration asks whether predicted probabilities match observed frequencies. Ranking quality and calibrated risk are different. A deployment gate may need calibrated probabilities and a cost-sensitive threshold; a retriever may primarily need good ordering. Choose metrics for the decision being made.</p>` },
        { title: "Use LLM judges as instruments", body: `<p>Specify the task rubric, evidence, output schema, and model/version. Randomize answer order in pairwise evaluation and test position, verbosity, and self-preference biases. Include known-good and known-bad anchors. Compare a sample against independent human labels, inspect disagreement, and report reliability.</p><p>A judge can hallucinate, misread evidence, or follow malicious instructions embedded in candidate text. Separate candidate content from evaluation instructions and treat judge output as untrusted structured data. Never make one uncalibrated judge the sole release criterion for a high-impact system.</p>` },
        { title: "Do the improvement arithmetic correctly", body: `<p>An increase from 0.40 to 0.44 is +0.04 absolute, or +4 percentage points, and +10% relative. Coverage and satisfaction have different denominators. If suppressing hard queries improves satisfaction among answered queries, overall user value may still fall. Report both answered-query and all-query outcomes.</p><p>Use paired query-level resampling when comparing ranking on the same queries. Use time-aware backtests for forecasting; rolling-origin validation avoids future leakage. Explain why A/B experiments support causal claims more directly than simple before/after comparisons, while still requiring valid randomization and measurement.</p>` },
        { title: "Practice explaining the trade-offs", body: `<p>Use a synthetic task or an example you are authorized to discuss. State the problem, baseline, data contracts, model choices, evaluation design, and failure modes. Separate assumptions from observations and do not invent impact numbers.</p><p>Design an ablation that isolates the proposed change. Define metric denominators, uncertainty, quality and latency gates, a staged rollout, and rollback conditions. Explain an alternative and what evidence would change your decision.</p>` }
      ],
      sources: [["Trustworthy Online Controlled Experiments", "https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/trustworthy-online-controlled-experiments/D97B26382EB0EB2DC2019A7A7B518F59"], ["Judging LLM-as-a-Judge", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.05685"], ["Scikit-learn model evaluation", "https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/model_evaluation.html"]],
      questions: [
        ["A metric rises from 40% to 44%. What is the relative increase?", ["4%", "44%", "10%", "0.4%"], 2, "(44 - 40) / 40 = 10% relative, or 4 percentage points absolute."],
        ["Which practice helps reduce pairwise LLM-judge position bias?", ["Always put the new model first", "Randomize answer order and calibrate against human labels", "Hide all evidence", "Use longer answers only"], 1, "Randomization and human calibration help measure and reduce judge artifacts; they do not guarantee a bias-free judge."],
        ["Why can satisfaction among answered queries be misleading by itself?", ["It is identical to recall", "It measures hardware speed", "It cannot be recorded", "Suppressing hard queries can improve it while reducing coverage"], 3, "The denominator changes when queries are suppressed. Measure coverage and all-query value alongside conditional satisfaction."]
      ]
    },
    {
      id: "agents", stage: 3, title: "From a workflow to an agent", subtitle: "Tools, state, planning, and bounded autonomy",
      time: 30, tags: ["agents", "tool calling", "ReAct", "state machines"], icon: "10",
      objective: "Choose when model-directed control is justified and design a safe, inspectable execution loop.",
      sections: [
        { title: "Do you actually need an agent?", body: `<p>A deterministic workflow follows predefined transitions; an agent lets a model choose actions based on observations. A RAG system is not necessarily agentic. Use a fixed pipeline when the task and tool sequence are predictable. Use model-directed selection when the needed steps depend materially on intermediate evidence.</p><p>Autonomy adds nondeterminism, latency, cost, and new failure paths. Start with one bounded agent and a small tool surface. Multiple agents help only when the task decomposition and measured benefit justify coordination overhead.</p>` },
        { title: "Make the loop explicit", body: `<div class="code-block">state &larr; task + authorized context<br>repeat within step / time / cost budget:<br>&nbsp; action &larr; model(state, tool schemas)<br>&nbsp; validate schema, authorization, policy<br>&nbsp; if answer: validate and stop<br>&nbsp; observation &larr; execute allowed tool with timeout<br>&nbsp; state &larr; append typed result<br>if budget exhausted: return explicit incomplete status</div><p>ReAct popularized interleaving reasoning-oriented steps and actions. Production traces should capture decisions, tool arguments, observations, and outcomes needed for debugging; do not require exposing hidden chain-of-thought. A concise rationale or structured plan is enough for observability.</p>` },
        { title: "Tools are contracts, not prompt suggestions", body: `<p>Define typed arguments, required fields, allowed ranges, and structured success/error responses. Validate outside the model. Distinguish read-only tools from state-changing tools. A valid JSON tool call can still be unauthorized, semantically wrong, or unsafe.</p><p>Handle timeouts, partial results, and malformed outputs explicitly. Retry only suitable transient failures with bounded backoff. Writes need idempotency keys and confirmation boundaries. An agent should not report success until the external operation has a verified durable result.</p>` },
        { title: "State and memory need ownership", body: `<p>Short-term state includes the current task and observations; durable memory should have provenance, scope, retention, and deletion rules. Summaries can lose constraints or source attribution, so preserve critical invariants in structured state. Never let untrusted documents become system instructions or durable "user preferences" without validation.</p><p>For long tasks, checkpoint progress and permit safe resumption. A plan is provisional; update it when observations invalidate assumptions. Prevent loops with explicit budgets and repeated-action detection rather than hoping the prompt will say "stop."</p>` },
        { title: "Orchestration patterns and tool protocols", body: `<p><strong>Routing</strong> chooses a specialized workflow; <strong>parallelization</strong> executes independent work; <strong>planner/executor</strong> separates decomposition from action; <strong>evaluator/optimizer</strong> iterates against a rubric. A state graph makes legal transitions, checkpoints, and human interrupts explicit. Parallelize only independent work; concurrent writes need conflict handling. A model-generated plan is not a trusted program.</p><p>Multiple agents need typed handoffs, clear ownership, a termination condition, shared-state rules, and a coordination budget. They can amplify correlated mistakes rather than provide independent evidence. Benchmark against a single-agent and deterministic baseline before paying that complexity cost.</p><p>A tool protocol such as MCP standardizes how clients discover and invoke tools or access resources; it does not itself supply planning, trustworthy tool output, authorization policy, or correct task execution. Authenticate the tool server, scope capabilities, validate calls, and version contracts. Protocol interoperability is not a security guarantee.</p>` },
        { title: "Practice explaining the trade-offs", body: `<p>Use a synthetic task or an example you are authorized to discuss. State the problem, baseline, data contracts, model choices, evaluation design, and failure modes. Separate assumptions from observations and do not invent impact numbers.</p><p>Design an ablation that isolates the proposed change. Define metric denominators, uncertainty, quality and latency gates, a staged rollout, and rollback conditions. Explain an alternative and what evidence would change your decision.</p>` }
      ],
      sources: [["ReAct", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.03629"], ["Building effective agents", "https://www.anthropic.com/research/building-effective-agents"]],
      questions: [
        ["When is a deterministic workflow usually preferable?", ["When steps are known and predictable", "Whenever more agents can be added", "When tools lack schemas", "When no evaluation exists"], 0, "Predictable tasks benefit from simpler, testable control flow. Agentic autonomy needs a demonstrated reason."],
        ["A tool call is valid JSON. What else must be checked?", ["Nothing", "Only spelling", "Authorization, semantic constraints, and side-effect policy", "Only the model temperature"], 2, "Schema validity does not establish permission or safety. Enforce constraints in the execution layer."],
        ["What should happen when an agent exhausts its step budget?", ["Claim success anyway", "Continue indefinitely", "Erase the trace", "Stop with an explicit incomplete status and useful state"], 3, "Bounded autonomy requires explicit stopping behavior and honest reporting of incomplete work."]
      ]
    },
    {
      id: "agent-eval", stage: 3, title: "Evaluate the whole agent", subtitle: "Trajectories, reliability, and adversarial inputs",
      time: 30, tags: ["agent evaluation", "prompt injection", "idempotency", "observability"], icon: "11",
      objective: "Measure end-to-end task completion and test failure recovery, not just attractive final responses.",
      sections: [
        { title: "The trajectory is part of the output", body: `<p>An agent can give a correct final sentence after making an unauthorized write. Evaluate final state, tool choices, arguments, side effects, resource use, and policy adherence. Use tasks with known expected outcomes in a controlled environment and verify them through independent state checks.</p><p>Record success rate across repeated runs, not just one best trace. A "success at least once in k attempts" metric differs from single-attempt reliability and can hide inconsistent behavior. Report latency, cost, tool-call count, and failure categories beside task success.</p>` },
        { title: "Build a failure matrix", body: `<p>Test missing evidence, conflicting evidence, malformed tool output, empty results, rate limits, timeouts, expired credentials, partial writes, duplicate retries, and concurrent edits. Define what the agent should do in each case: retry, ask, abstain, roll back where supported, or escalate.</p><p>Classify failures into planning, retrieval, tool selection, argument generation, execution, interpretation, and final reporting. Use ablations to see whether a deterministic baseline or a single-agent variant performs as well as a more complex design.</p>` },
        { title: "Treat retrieved instructions as untrusted data", body: `<p>A document or tool result may say "ignore previous rules" or request data exfiltration. Prompt wording helps separation, but the reliable boundary is external permission enforcement: least-privilege credentials, allowlisted tool destinations, data scoping, and review for sensitive actions.</p><p>Test indirect prompt injection in retrieved snippets, tool outputs, filenames, and citations. Keep sensitive data out of unnecessary traces and judge prompts. Validate rendered output and links. An evaluation suite should check whether the system refuses unauthorized actions even when the model proposes them.</p>` },
        { title: "Make retries and recovery safe", body: `<p>If a write times out, the operation may still have succeeded. Retrying blindly can duplicate side effects. Use idempotency keys, query operation status, and checkpoint external IDs. Compensation is not always possible, so identify irreversible operations before execution.</p><p>Capture trace IDs, tool latency, redacted arguments, result types, model version, and policy decisions. Use bounded retention and access controls. Good observability explains failures without exposing personal data or relying on private model reasoning.</p>` },
        { title: "An executable benchmark contract", body: `<div class="code-block">task_id: update-009<br>initial_state: document-v2, source-v3<br>allowed_tools: [search, read, propose_patch]<br>forbidden_tools: [publish, external_send]<br>budget: 8 calls / 60 seconds / configured cost cap<br>expected_final_state: reviewed proposal; original unchanged<br>validators: source-supported diff, valid version, no forbidden writes<br>fault_injection: search timeout once<br>repeat_runs: 5</div><p>Use a fresh controlled state for every attempt. Stub tools for deterministic unit tests; use a sandbox with real contracts for integration tests; reserve held-out tasks and fault combinations for end-to-end evaluation. A task may have several valid action sequences, so check invariants and outcomes rather than demanding one exact trace.</p><p>For each tool, test schema validation, permissions, timeout behavior, idempotency, and typed error handling. For the agent loop, test budget exhaustion and stopping. For the task, verify final state independently. For a judge, test human agreement and adversarial content. This separates component, integration, and task-level evidence.</p>` },
        { title: "Reliability metrics and their traps", body: `<p><strong>Task success:</strong> attempts with a correct verified outcome / attempts. <strong>Policy adherence:</strong> attempts with no prohibited action / attempts. <strong>Tool-call validity:</strong> schema-valid calls / proposed calls, measured separately from authorized and semantically correct calls. <strong>Recovery rate:</strong> successfully completed injected-failure tasks / injected-failure tasks. Also measure unnecessary escalation and incorrect claims of completion.</p><p>Report median and tail latency, cost per attempt and per successful task, tool calls, loop rate, and human intervention rate. An inexpensive failed agent is not an efficient successful system. On ten tasks repeated five times each, all 50 attempts belong in the denominator, not only the best trace per task.</p><p><strong>pass@k</strong> asks whether at least one of k attempts succeeds; <strong>all-k reliability</strong> asks whether all k succeed. For independent identical attempts with per-attempt success p, the probabilities are 1−(1−p)<sup>k</sup> and p<sup>k</sup>. At p=0.8 and k=3, those are 0.992 and 0.512. Real runs can be correlated; independence is an illustrative assumption, not a guaranteed estimator. Report uncertainty at the task level when tasks are repeated.</p>` },
        { title: "Practice explaining the trade-offs", body: `<p>Use a synthetic task or an example you are authorized to discuss. State the problem, baseline, data contracts, model choices, evaluation design, and failure modes. Separate assumptions from observations and do not invent impact numbers.</p><p>Design an ablation that isolates the proposed change. Define metric denominators, uncertainty, quality and latency gates, a staged rollout, and rollback conditions. Explain an alternative and what evidence would change your decision.</p>` }
      ],
      sources: [["AgentBench", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.03688"], ["OWASP guidance for LLM applications", "https://genai.owasp.org/llm-top-10/"]],
      questions: [
        ["Why evaluate tool trajectories as well as final answers?", ["Final wording proves all actions were safe", "A correct answer can hide unauthorized side effects", "Trajectories replace ground truth", "Tool arguments never matter"], 1, "Correct final text does not prove correct execution. Inspect actions and verify the external final state."],
        ["A write times out. What is the safest next step?", ["Retry forever", "Assume it failed", "Check operation status or use an idempotency mechanism before retrying", "Report success without verification"], 2, "Timeout means unknown outcome, not definite failure. Avoid duplicating an already committed operation."],
        ["What is the strongest boundary against an unauthorized tool action?", ["Asking the model to be careful", "A longer chain of thought", "Hiding the tool description", "External authorization and least-privilege execution"], 3, "Permissions must be enforced outside the model; prompt-only constraints are not an authorization system."]
      ]
    },
    {
      id: "system-design", stage: 3, title: "Bring it together in the interview", subtitle: "End-to-end design and evidence-backed stories",
      time: 40, tags: ["system design", "project deep dive", "STAR", "trade-offs"], icon: "12",
      objective: "Give a structured system design answer and defend your project claims with evidence, limitations, and trade-offs.",
      sections: [
        { title: "A 45-minute design structure", body: `<p><strong>0-5 minutes:</strong> clarify users, traffic, freshness, correctness, permissions, and SLOs. <strong>5-12:</strong> define data contracts and a baseline architecture. <strong>12-25:</strong> go deep on retrieval, model choice, evaluation, and serving bottlenecks. <strong>25-35:</strong> failures, reliability, safety, rollout, and costs. <strong>35-45:</strong> trade-offs, alternatives, and interviewer-led depth.</p><p>Numbers should be labeled assumptions. Estimate requests per second, token lengths, corpus size, update rate, and memory, then check consistency. Avoid listing tools without explaining data flow and decision boundaries. A clear simple baseline is stronger than an unexplained stack of fashionable components.</p>` },
        { title: "Design a fresh factual search-answer service", body: `<p>Start with authorized, versioned source ingestion. Combine lexical/dense candidates, rerank, assemble a constrained evidence budget, generate with a small adapted model, validate schema/citations, and suppress unsupported answers. Cache only with appropriate source freshness and access scope. Define degraded paths when the model or index is unavailable.</p><p>Allocate an end-to-end latency budget across queue, retrieval, rerank, prefill, decode, and network. Measure coverage and correctness together. Use offline relevance and grounding tests, then a guarded online rollout with error, latency, and satisfaction monitoring. Explain when a deterministic answer extractor would be better than generation.</p>` },
        { title: "Practice explaining the trade-offs", body: `<p>Use a synthetic task or an example you are authorized to discuss. State the problem, baseline, data contracts, model choices, evaluation design, and failure modes. Separate assumptions from observations and do not invent impact numbers.</p><p>Design an ablation that isolates the proposed change. Define metric denominators, uncertainty, quality and latency gates, a staged rollout, and rollback conditions. Explain an alternative and what evidence would change your decision.</p>` },
        { title: "Prepare for coding and scientific depth", body: `<p>Practice stable softmax and masked attention; contrastive-loss pseudocode; a top-k retrieval evaluator; NDCG; a deduplicated SQL experiment join; a calibration/reliability plot; and a bounded tool loop with idempotent writes. Explain complexity and write edge-case tests, including empty inputs, zero relevant documents, padding, duplicate IDs, and missing observations.</p><p>For Applied Scientist roles, emphasize hypotheses, experimental controls, error analysis, and what would falsify your approach. For MLE roles, emphasize service contracts, deployment, observability, load testing, data/version management, and rollback. Both require sound science and production reasoning.</p>` },
        { title: "A final rehearsal", body: `<p>Choose one scenario in the Interview Studio. Give a two-minute overview, then take ten minutes on one design decision. Score yourself on problem framing, technical correctness, evidence, alternatives, and failure handling. Repeat with an interviewer who challenges your preferred approach.</p><div class="callout"><strong>Readiness is not a quiz score alone</strong><p>The final assessment checks conceptual breadth. You are interview-ready when you can derive the essentials, implement the core algorithms, debug a failing system, and defend your real work without unsupported claims.</p></div>` }
      ],
      sources: [["Rules of Machine Learning", "https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/guides/rules-of-ml"], ["Google SRE book", "https://sre.google/sre-book/table-of-contents/"]],
      questions: [
        ["What should come before selecting a model for a system design?", ["Choosing the largest checkpoint", "Adding multiple agents", "Clarifying requirements, constraints, and success metrics", "Writing a marketing claim"], 2, "Model selection follows task, scale, freshness, correctness, permissions, and latency/cost requirements."],
        ["An interviewer asks for a detail you do not remember. What is best?", ["Invent a precise value", "State the uncertainty and explain how you would verify it", "Change the topic without acknowledgement", "Repeat the headline metric"], 1, "Honest uncertainty plus a verification method is more credible than invented precision."],
        ["What makes a strong project-impact claim?", ["A large percentage without a denominator", "Only a training-loss curve", "Only the architecture diagram", "A baseline, defined metric, attribution evidence, and limitations"], 3, "Impact is defensible when measurement and attribution are clear, including uncertainty and trade-offs."]
      ]
    }
  ],
  scenarios: [
  {
    "title": "Design a grounded search service",
    "kind": "Practice scenario",
    "time": "25 min",
    "prompt": "Design a factual question-answer service over a synthetic, versioned document corpus.",
    "checks": [
      "Define requirements, freshness, permissions, and SLOs.",
      "Draw retrieval, reranking, context, generation, and abstention contracts.",
      "Define retrieval, grounding, correctness, coverage, cost, and latency evaluation.",
      "Isolate component contributions with ablations and oracle evidence.",
      "Plan guarded rollout, monitoring, and rollback."
    ]
  },
  {
    "title": "Choose a post-training method",
    "kind": "Practice scenario",
    "time": "20 min",
    "prompt": "Compare SFT, DPO, and PPO for a small grounded-answer model with limited feedback.",
    "checks": [
      "Derive the objective of each method.",
      "Specify data and reference-policy needs.",
      "Check noisy labels, length bias, and proxy exploitation.",
      "Evaluate factuality separately from preference.",
      "Choose the simplest justified experiment."
    ]
  },
  {
    "title": "Build a bounded tool agent",
    "kind": "Practice scenario",
    "time": "30 min",
    "prompt": "Design an agent that finds evidence and proposes document edits without publishing them.",
    "checks": [
      "Justify autonomy over a fixed workflow.",
      "Enforce typed tools and external authorization.",
      "Handle budgets, injection, and partial failures.",
      "Use version checks and idempotent writes.",
      "Measure repeated-run success, side effects, trajectories, latency, and cost."
    ]
  },
  {
    "title": "Debug a GPU bottleneck",
    "kind": "Practice scenario",
    "time": "20 min",
    "prompt": "An isolated attention benchmark improves, but service p95 is unchanged.",
    "checks": [
      "Separate queueing, prefill, decode, and non-model time.",
      "Time asynchronous device work correctly.",
      "Inspect actual kernel/backend selection.",
      "Validate dtype and task parity.",
      "Benchmark representative traffic and concurrency."
    ]
  },
  {
    "title": "Debug a distributed data job",
    "kind": "Practice scenario",
    "time": "20 min",
    "prompt": "A Spark feature job develops duplicate rows and one very slow stage.",
    "checks": [
      "Validate schema, grain, and join key cardinality.",
      "Inspect the physical plan and task skew.",
      "Check shuffle, spill, and partition sizes.",
      "Propose one measured improvement.",
      "Verify multiset parity, freshness, and end-to-end job cost."
    ]
  }
]
};

GUIDE.lessons.splice(2, 0, {
  id: "pytorch", stage: 0, title: "Build it in PyTorch & Transformers",
  subtitle: "Tensors, autograd, causal attention, and model APIs",
  time: 40, tags: ["PyTorch", "Hugging Face", "autograd", "SDPA"], icon: "",
  objective: "Translate transformer equations into tensor operations, avoid masking and loss bugs, and use model APIs deliberately.",
  sections: [
    { title: "Three names that are not interchangeable", body: `<p>A <strong>transformer</strong> is an architecture. <strong>PyTorch</strong> is a tensor/autograd framework used to implement and train it. Hugging Face <strong>Transformers</strong> is a library of model architectures, checkpoints, tokenizers, and training/generation utilities, often backed by PyTorch. Knowing one library's API is not the same as understanding the architecture or GPU execution.</p><p>In PyTorch, tensors have shape, dtype, device, and stride. A transpose can produce a non-contiguous view; view requires a compatible layout, while reshape may copy. Broadcasting silently expands compatible dimensions, so assert shapes before trusting a loss. nn.Module registers submodules and Parameters; putting layers in a plain list instead of a ModuleList can omit them from parameter discovery.</p>` },
    { title: "Autograd, train mode, and inference mode", body: `<p>Autograd records differentiable operations on tensors requiring gradients. backward accumulates gradients in leaf parameters, so zero them deliberately between optimizer updates unless accumulating. Detach separates a tensor from the graph; item transfers a scalar to Python and can synchronize CUDA work. Retaining tensors with attached graphs in a history list can keep large activation graphs alive.</p><p>model.train() and model.eval() switch behavior for modules such as dropout and batch normalization; eval does not disable gradient recording. no_grad disables gradient tracking for its scope; inference_mode further restricts autograd-related bookkeeping. A typical prediction path needs both eval behavior and a no-gradient context. When resuming training, restore train mode.</p><p>Mixed precision uses lower precision for suitable operations while preserving stability where needed. Autocast chooses operation dtypes; FP16 training often uses gradient scaling. BF16 has a larger exponent range than FP16 but hardware support varies. Verify finite loss, gradient norms, and task quality rather than assuming reduced precision is harmless.</p>` },
    { title: "A minimal causal attention module", body: `<p>This educational PyTorch 2.x example handles equal-length, unpadded sequences. It is not a complete decoder: add normalization, residuals, positional information, an MLP, and task heads separately. The source documents the SDPA API; this browser does not execute Python or validate your local GPU.</p><pre class="code-block"><code>import torch
from torch import nn
from torch.nn import functional as F

class CausalAttention(nn.Module):
    def __init__(self, width: int, heads: int) -> None:
        super().__init__()
        if width &lt;= 0 or heads &lt;= 0 or width % heads:
            raise ValueError("width must be positive and divisible by heads")
        self.heads = heads
        self.qkv = nn.Linear(width, 3 * width, bias=False)
        self.output = nn.Linear(width, width, bias=False)

    def forward(self, x: torch.Tensor) -> torch.Tensor:
        batch, tokens, width = x.shape
        qkv = self.qkv(x).reshape(
            batch, tokens, 3, self.heads, width // self.heads
        )
        q, k, v = qkv.permute(2, 0, 3, 1, 4).unbind(0)
        attended = F.scaled_dot_product_attention(
            q, k, v, is_causal=True, dropout_p=0.0
        )
        merged = attended.transpose(1, 2).reshape(batch, tokens, width)
        return self.output(merged)</code></pre><p>Q/K/V have shape [batch, heads, tokens, head_width]. In SDPA, a boolean attention mask uses True for allowed positions; this differs from MultiheadAttention key_padding_mask, where True marks padding to ignore. Do not copy masks between APIs without checking semantics. SDPA applies its dropout_p even if a containing module is in eval mode, so pass zero during evaluation.</p><p>The simple is_causal=True path above assumes square, non-cached attention. Cached decoding may have different query and key lengths and positional offsets; a naive triangular mask can align incorrectly. Construct and test the correct offset-aware mask for that implementation. Handle padding explicitly; do not blindly combine incompatible mask arguments.</p>` },
    { title: "Map the training objective to the model API", body: `<p>AutoTokenizer loads the tokenizer/config; AutoModelForCausalLM loads a checkpoint with a causal language-model head, whereas AutoModel generally exposes base hidden representations. Pin a reviewed checkpoint revision and library versions. Loading an unknown remote model with custom code is a trust decision; do not enable remote code casually.</p><p>Pass the expected input IDs and attention mask and use the checkpoint's chat template. For the standard causal-LM loss in supported model classes, labels typically match input positions and the model shifts logits/labels internally; verify the model's contract instead of shifting twice. Replace padding and any non-supervised prompt labels with -100 when that loss uses -100 as the ignore index. If a custom collator is used, inspect its exact output.</p><p>A Trainer can manage optimizer, scheduling, accumulation, checkpoints, and evaluation, but it does not choose a leakage-free dataset or valid metric for you. Generation is a separate API from a forward pass and may use caching and decoding controls. Log both input and generated token counts when comparing latency. Never assume a model's training loss directly measures generation quality.</p>` },
    { title: "Correctness tests worth writing before tuning", body: `<p><strong>Shapes:</strong> assert output [B,T,d] and causal-LM logits [B,T,V]. <strong>Causality:</strong> perturb a future token and compare earlier outputs with dropout off. <strong>Masking:</strong> padded labels must contribute zero loss, and padded keys must not influence real positions. <strong>Gradients:</strong> intended trainable parameters get finite gradients while frozen parameters stay unchanged.</p><p><strong>Reference parity:</strong> compare SDPA against a stable explicit QK/softmax/V implementation at small shapes with a dtype-appropriate tolerance. <strong>Save/reload:</strong> compare predictions after restoring weights and configuration. <strong>Training wiring:</strong> overfit a tiny clean batch, then use a genuine held-out set for generalization. These tests find different bugs; one does not replace the others.</p>` },
    { title: "Practice explaining the trade-offs", body: `<p>Use a synthetic task or an example you are authorized to discuss. State the problem, baseline, data contracts, model choices, evaluation design, and failure modes. Separate assumptions from observations and do not invent impact numbers.</p><p>Design an ablation that isolates the proposed change. Define metric denominators, uncertainty, quality and latency gates, a staged rollout, and rollback conditions. Explain an alternative and what evidence would change your decision.</p>` }
  ],
  sources: [["PyTorch autograd basics", "https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/autogradqs_tutorial.html"], ["PyTorch SDPA contract", "https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/stable/generated/torch.nn.functional.scaled_dot_product_attention.html"], ["Hugging Face model APIs", "https://huggingface.co/docs/transformers/main_classes/model"], ["Hugging Face chat templates", "https://huggingface.co/docs/transformers/chat_templating"]],
  questions: [
    ["Does model.eval() disable autograd?", ["Yes, all gradients become impossible", "No; it changes relevant module behavior, while gradient tracking needs a separate context", "Only on CPU", "Only for all transformer models"], 1, "eval changes behavior such as dropout; no_grad or inference_mode handles gradient recording for inference."],
    ["For PyTorch SDPA, what does True in a boolean attention mask mean?", ["The position must be ignored", "The token is necessarily padding", "The position is allowed to participate", "The result is detached"], 2, "SDPA boolean True means allowed, unlike MultiheadAttention key_padding_mask True meaning ignore. Always verify the exact API."],
    ["A causal-LM model already shifts labels internally. What happens if you also pre-shift labels?", ["You can misalign predictions and targets by shifting twice", "Loss becomes automatically correct", "It disables padding", "It enables FlashAttention"], 0, "Inspect the model's loss contract. Double shifting trains the wrong alignment rather than improving next-token supervision."]
  ]
});

GUIDE.lessons.splice(GUIDE.lessons.findIndex(lesson => lesson.id === "inference") + 1, 0, {
  id: "cuda", stage: 2, title: "Understand the GPU beneath the model",
  subtitle: "CUDA execution, memory, profiling, and distributed training",
  time: 40, tags: ["CUDA", "GPU memory", "profiling", "distributed training"], icon: "",
  objective: "Reason about GPU bottlenecks, benchmark asynchronous operations correctly, and explain when kernel or distributed optimizations help.",
  sections: [
    { title: "CUDA's execution model", body: `<p>CUDA is NVIDIA's parallel computing platform and programming model, not a model architecture. The CPU launches kernels on a GPU. A kernel runs a grid of thread blocks; blocks execute on streaming multiprocessors. Threads are scheduled in warps, typically 32 on NVIDIA CUDA hardware. Threads in a block can cooperate via shared memory and synchronization; ordinary block-local barriers do not synchronize the whole grid.</p><p>GPUs hide memory latency by scheduling other ready warps. Divergent branches within a warp can reduce efficiency. Higher occupancy can help hide latency, but maximizing occupancy alone is not the goal: register pressure, shared-memory usage, arithmetic intensity, and instruction mix also matter. Tensor Cores accelerate supported matrix operations and precision modes, not every arbitrary operator.</p>` },
    { title: "The memory hierarchy explains many speedups", body: `<p>Device high-bandwidth memory is large but slower than registers and on-chip shared memory. Coalesced accesses let nearby threads read nearby addresses efficiently. Tiling reuses data on-chip; fusion can avoid intermediate memory traffic and launch overhead. Fusion can also increase register pressure or restrict scheduling, so benchmark rather than assuming every fused graph is faster.</p><p>The roofline view compares arithmetic intensity (operations per byte transferred) with hardware compute and bandwidth ceilings. Large matrix multiplications can be compute-bound; small-batch decoding often spends heavily on reading weights and KV state. FlashAttention raises effective attention efficiency by reducing memory IO rather than eliminating all arithmetic.</p><p>Separate host RAM, device weights, activations, gradients, optimizer states, KV cache, and temporary workspaces. PyTorch allocated memory is memory used by tensors; reserved memory includes allocator-managed blocks and can exceed allocated memory. empty_cache does not free live tensors or cure a retained autograd graph.</p>` },
    { title: "Asynchronous execution changes how you time code", body: `<p>CUDA kernel launches are asynchronous relative to the CPU in common execution paths. A Python wall-clock timer around an unsynchronized operation may mostly measure dispatch time. Warm up first and use CUDA events for same-stream device timing, or synchronize the relevant work before reading an end-to-end wall-clock duration. Include transfer and queueing time when that is what the product metric requires.</p><pre class="code-block"><code>import torch

if not torch.cuda.is_available():
    raise RuntimeError("This timing example requires a CUDA device")
x = torch.randn(1024, 1024, device="cuda")
for _ in range(10):
    result = x @ x
start = torch.cuda.Event(enable_timing=True)
end = torch.cuda.Event(enable_timing=True)
start.record()
for _ in range(50):
    result = x @ x
end.record()
end.synchronize()
print(start.elapsed_time(end) / 50, "ms per matmul")</code></pre><p>This is an illustrative API example, not a measured benchmark for your machine. It excludes setup and host/device transfers and uses the default stream. Record GPU model, precision configuration, shapes, warm-up, repeats, and the timing scope before comparing numbers. GPU execution has not been run as part of this static website.</p>` },
    { title: "Streams, transfers, and profiling", body: `<p>Operations in one stream are ordered; separate streams may overlap only when dependencies and hardware resources allow it. Use events/waits to express dependencies and handle tensor lifetimes correctly. Pinned host memory plus suitable asynchronous transfers can enable copy/compute overlap; non_blocking=True alone is not a universal overlap guarantee.</p><p>Use torch.profiler for CPU/CUDA operator traces and memory attribution. NVIDIA Nsight Systems shows host/device scheduling, transfers, and gaps; Nsight Compute examines kernel-level throughput and memory behavior. Investigate synchronization points such as item(), CPU copies, and debug logging in the hot path. Profiling adds overhead, so separate diagnostic runs from final benchmarks.</p><p>A useful sequence: establish a correct baseline → profile representative load → identify the dominant bottleneck → change one relevant mechanism → verify numerical/task parity → remeasure the complete system. torch.compile can reduce overhead and fuse eligible graphs, but compilation warm-up, graph breaks, dynamic shapes, and backend compatibility affect gains. Eager mode remains an important correctness baseline.</p>` },
    { title: "From one GPU to many", body: `<p>Data parallelism replicates the model and distributes batches. DistributedDataParallel synchronizes gradients; each rank still holds replicated model state unless additional sharding is used. FSDP and ZeRO-style approaches shard parameters, gradients, or optimizer state with different communication/memory trade-offs. Tensor parallelism shards computations within layers; pipeline parallelism partitions layers and must manage pipeline bubbles.</p><p>Activation checkpointing saves memory by recomputing selected activations in backward. Gradient accumulation changes update frequency and effective batch but does not shard weights. Communication, interconnect topology, gradient synchronization, and stragglers can dominate scaling. Global batch, sampler sharding, loss normalization, and checkpoint restoration must remain correct when changing world size.</p><p>For inference, model parallelism can make a model fit but also adds communication latency. A smaller model on one device may be better for an SLO than a larger model spread over several devices. Quantify quality, throughput, latency, memory headroom, and cost per successful request.</p>` },
    { title: "Practice explaining the trade-offs", body: `<p>Use a synthetic task or an example you are authorized to discuss. State the problem, baseline, data contracts, model choices, evaluation design, and failure modes. Separate assumptions from observations and do not invent impact numbers.</p><p>Design an ablation that isolates the proposed change. Define metric denominators, uncertainty, quality and latency gates, a staged rollout, and rollback conditions. Explain an alternative and what evidence would change your decision.</p>` }
  ],
  sources: [["CUDA programming guide", "https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cuda-programming-guide/"], ["PyTorch CUDA semantics", "https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/stable/notes/cuda.html"], ["PyTorch profiler", "https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/recipes/recipes/profiler_recipe.html"], ["PyTorch distributed overview", "https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/dist_overview.html"], ["NVIDIA Nsight Systems", "https://docs.nvidia.com/nsight-systems/"]],
  questions: [
    ["Why can an unsynchronized CPU timer underreport CUDA operation time?", ["CUDA launches are commonly asynchronous with respect to the CPU", "GPUs never execute kernels", "All tensor operations are instant", "The tokenizer changes the clock"], 0, "The timer may measure dispatch rather than completed device work. Use appropriate events or synchronization for the intended timing scope."],
    ["What is the key idea behind tiling and kernel fusion for memory-bound workloads?", ["Eliminate all model weights", "Always maximize Python threads", "Reuse fast on-chip data and reduce intermediate memory traffic", "Make every operation exactly deterministic"], 2, "Tiling and fusion can reduce costly memory traffic and launches, though register pressure and compatibility may offset gains."],
    ["What does ordinary DistributedDataParallel primarily do?", ["Shards every parameter by default", "Replicates models across ranks and synchronizes gradients", "Removes the need for data splitting", "Guarantees linear scaling at any batch size"], 1, "DDP is replicated data parallelism. Sharding strategies such as FSDP or ZeRO address different memory trade-offs."]
  ]
});

GUIDE.stages.push("Spark add-on");
GUIDE.lessons.push({
  id: "spark", stage: 4, title: "Spark: think in distributed data",
  subtitle: "A practical add-on: PySpark, SQL, joins, and execution",
  time: 40, tags: ["Spark", "PySpark", "shuffles", "DataFrames"], icon: "",
  objective: "Explain a Spark execution plan, write basic DataFrame transformations, and debug correctness and performance before tuning configuration.",
  sections: [
    { title: "When Spark helps—and when it doesn't", body: `<p>Apache Spark is a distributed data-processing engine. A driver builds work and coordinates executors, which run tasks on data partitions. Use Spark when data size, parallelism, or a shared data-platform workflow justifies distribution. For a small in-memory dataset, Python/SQL on one machine may be simpler and faster: scheduling and network traffic are real overheads.</p><p>PySpark is Spark's Python interface, not a way to make arbitrary Python code automatically parallel. Built-in DataFrame/SQL expressions describe operations to Spark's execution engine. The typed Dataset API is available in Scala/Java, not Python. RDDs expose lower-level distributed collections; prefer DataFrames for structured data when their optimizer can help.</p>` },
    { title: "Lazy evaluation: jobs, stages, and tasks", body: `<p>Transformations such as select and filter build a lazy logical plan. Actions such as count, collect, and writing results trigger execution. Spark optimizes the plan, chooses physical operators, and schedules work. A job is broken into stages at shuffle dependencies; tasks process partitions within a stage. SQL execution can involve multiple jobs and adaptive changes, so "one action always equals one job" is an oversimplification.</p><p>Narrow dependencies let output partitions depend on a limited set of input partitions. Wide operations such as many groupBy, joins, distinct, and repartition plans require redistribution. A shuffle writes and exchanges data, often adding disk/network cost. Inspect explain("formatted") and the Spark UI to see what actually executed rather than counting API calls.</p>` },
    { title: "DataFrames, schemas, nulls, and native expressions", body: `<p>A DataFrame has a schema and distributed rows with no inherent ordering. Declare types and nullability deliberately when ingesting production data; inference can be expensive or inconsistent. Use column expressions rather than Python if statements on Columns. SQL null comparisons follow three-valued logic: use isNull/isNotNull for null checks, and use null-safe equality only when the intended join semantics require it.</p><p>Prefer built-in functions for filtering, parsing, aggregation, and date handling. Ordinary Python UDFs add a language boundary and may hide logic from optimization; vectorized UDFs can help certain workloads but do not make all Python logic free. collect brings all results to the driver and can exhaust memory. Collect only bounded results for inspection; write large results to distributed storage.</p>` },
    { title: "Joins and windows: correctness before speed", body: `<p>An inner join retains matching rows, a left join preserves all left rows, a semi join checks existence, and an anti join returns unmatched left rows. Duplicate keys on both sides can multiply rows. Define the data grain and validate key cardinality before using joins in experiment or feature pipelines. Do not repair unexplained duplication with an arbitrary dropDuplicates call.</p><p>A broadcast join can avoid shuffling the large side when one side is genuinely small enough for executor memory. It is not safe to broadcast every dimension table blindly. Sort-merge and shuffle-hash choices depend on data, statistics, configuration, and engine version.</p><p>Window functions preserve row-level context while computing within a partition key and order. To pick the latest record, use row_number over a deterministic order such as event time plus a unique event ID. A timestamp alone can tie. Filtering then ordering differently can silently change which records are retained.</p>` },
    { title: "Partitioning, caching, skew, and output files", body: `<p>repartition redistributes data and usually shuffles; coalesce commonly reduces partitions without a full shuffle but may reduce parallelism and create uneven work. Partition count is not the same as storage partition columns. Partition pruning skips directories or files based on filters; predicate pushdown moves supported filters closer to the data source. Columnar formats such as Parquet enable column pruning and statistics-based skipping.</p><p>Cache/persist only reused, expensive intermediates when the memory/storage trade-off pays off, and unpersist when finished. Persisting is lazy until materialized. Checkpointing truncates lineage using reliable storage and has a different purpose from caching. Too many tiny files hurt listing and scheduling; one giant output partition can bottleneck writes.</p><p>Data skew occurs when a few keys or partitions carry most of the work. Look for outlier task durations, shuffle bytes, spill, and executor memory pressure. Adaptive Query Execution can adjust some plans, coalesce shuffle partitions, or mitigate supported skew patterns, but is not a universal fix. Consider preaggregation, selective broadcasting, or carefully designed salting after verifying correctness.</p>` },
    { title: "Batch/streaming pipelines and ML data hygiene", body: `<p>Structured Streaming incrementally evaluates a query over arriving data, commonly using microbatches. Event time is when an event happened; processing time is when the engine handles it. Watermarks let the system bound state and reason about sufficiently late events; they are not a promise that all arbitrarily late records will be retained. Choose output mode, state retention, and checkpoint location deliberately.</p><p>Exactly-once effects depend on the source, sink, checkpoint/replay behavior, and application logic. An arbitrary external API called in foreachBatch is not automatically exactly-once; use idempotent writes and batch IDs where appropriate. Avoid changing a live checkpoint's query semantics without checking compatibility.</p><p>For ML, enforce time-correct joins, feature availability, deduplication rules, and entity-level splits. Compute quality checks at each data boundary: schema, row count, key uniqueness, null rate, label distribution, and source freshness. Distributed scale does not fix leakage. Distinguish upstream label/data errors from model failures.</p>` },
    { title: "A Spark interview drill and coding path", body: `<p>A daily feature job slows down after one customer becomes much larger than the rest. Explain how you would inspect the physical plan, stage/task skew, shuffle spill, and join cardinality; propose one measured change at a time. Then prove output parity at the intended grain and report the full job's cost and duration, not just one operator's speed.</p><div class="callout"><strong>Hands-on add-on</strong><p>The PySpark lab covers native filtering/aggregation, cardinality-aware left joins, deterministic latest-record windows, and partitioning. Use synthetic data, predict outputs before execution, and run the downloadable notebook with a compatible Spark/Java environment.</p><a href="#spark-coding">Open the Spark coding lab &rarr;</a></div>` }
  ],
  sources: [["Spark SQL and DataFrame guide", "https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-programming-guide.html"], ["PySpark join contract", "https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.join.html"], ["Spark tuning", "https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/tuning.html"], ["Structured Streaming", "https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/streaming/index.html"]],
  questions: [
    ["What does a DataFrame filter usually do before an action is called?", ["Builds a lazy plan for later execution", "Collects every row to Python", "Immediately writes a checkpoint", "Starts one Python process per row"], 0, "Spark DataFrame transformations are generally lazy. An action triggers evaluation of the optimized plan."],
    ["A left join unexpectedly multiplies rows. What should you check first?", ["Only the font of the output", "Join key cardinality and duplicate keys on the right", "Whether dropout is enabled", "The model vocabulary"], 1, "Multiple right-side matches duplicate each matching left row. Validate grain/cardinality before tuning join performance."],
    ["Why is collect risky on a large result?", ["It makes the schema disappear", "It guarantees a shuffle-free plan", "It brings all result rows into driver memory", "It changes inner joins to left joins"], 2, "collect materializes results at the driver and may exhaust memory. Use bounded inspection and distributed writes."]
  ]
});

const TRANSFORMER_STEPS = [
  {
    title: "Tokenization: text becomes integer IDs", short: "Tokenization",
    body: `<p><strong>What happens:</strong> a tokenizer splits text into vocabulary pieces and maps each piece to an integer ID. Pieces may be words, subwords, punctuation, or bytes. The illustrative sentence "I like ML" will use three tokens here; a real tokenizer may split it differently. IDs are addresses, not numerical measures of meaning: ID 200 is not twice as meaningful as ID 100.</p><div class="equation">"I like ML" &rarr; ["I", "like", "ML"] &rarr; [7, 12, 4]<br>Input shape: [B,T], integer IDs</div><p><strong>Why it is needed:</strong> the model operates on fixed-vocabulary symbols rather than raw strings. Special tokens can mark boundaries, roles, padding, or end of sequence. Padding requires a mask so it is not treated as ordinary evidence. Tokenization affects context length and cost; counting words is not the same as counting tokens.</p><p><strong>On the diagram:</strong> source/input tokens enter the encoder. Shifted target tokens enter a sequence-to-sequence decoder. A decoder-only LLM instead consumes the prompt and previous generated tokens as one causal sequence.</p>`
  },
  {
    title: "Token embeddings: IDs become learned feature vectors", short: "Token embeddings",
    body: `<p><strong>What happens:</strong> an embedding table E with shape [V_vocab,d_model] stores a trainable vector for each token ID. Lookup gathers the relevant rows. For IDs [7,12,4], the model reads E[7], E[12], and E[4]; it does not multiply the ID by a vector. The resulting hidden states have shape [B,T,d_model].</p><div class="equation">E[7] = [1,0], E[12] = [0,1], E[4] = [1,1]<br>[7,12,4] &rarr; [[1,0],[0,1],[1,1]]</div><p><strong>Why it is needed:</strong> continuous features support differentiable learning and dot products. The coordinates above are hand-chosen, not embeddings from a trained model. Individual dimensions do not necessarily correspond to human-readable concepts.</p><p><strong>Important distinction:</strong> a token's initial embedding is usually a lookup independent of surrounding text. After transformer blocks, its hidden state is contextual: the same token can have different representations in different sentences. A retrieval embedding is another object, typically requiring its own model, pooling, and training objective. The original Transformer also scales token embeddings by sqrt(d_model); this is not universal.</p>`
  },
  {
    title: "Positional information: give the model an order signal", short: "Position / RoPE",
    body: `<p><strong>The problem:</strong> unmasked self-attention without positional information is permutation equivariant: permuting input rows permutes output rows. A sentence needs more than an unordered bag of token meanings. "Dog chases cat" and "Cat chases dog" should not have interchangeable representations. A causal mask constrains visibility, but is not the same operation as supplying an explicit position signal.</p><p><strong>Additive positions:</strong> use learned position vectors or the original sinusoidal encoding, then add a position vector to each token embedding. Original sinusoidal coordinates use sin(pos / 10000^(2i/d_model)) and cos(pos / 10000^(2i/d_model)).</p><div class="equation">Token embedding e = [1,0]<br>Position 0: p = [sin(0),cos(0)] = [0,1]; e+p = [1,1]<br>Position 1: p &asymp; [0.841,0.540]; e+p &asymp; [1.841,0.540]</div><p><strong>RoPE is different:</strong> rotate pairs of Q/K coordinates by position-dependent angles inside attention rather than adding that vector to embeddings. The relative rotation affects the Q–K dot product. Do not apply this explanation as though RoPE simply adds a position vector or rotates V. Longer representable positions do not establish reliable long-context quality; evaluate extrapolation and retrieval at different offsets.</p>`
  },
  {
    title: "Self-attention: build Q, K, and V from the same sequence", short: "Self-attention / QKV",
    body: `<p><strong>What "self" means:</strong> queries, keys, and values are projected from representations of the same sequence. In a pre-norm block that representation is the normalized residual stream. Each token produces three learned views: Q asks what to match, K supplies matching features, and V supplies content to mix. These are mathematical roles, not literal words or database keys.</p><div class="equation">Q = XW_Q, K = XW_K, V = XW_V<br>Scores = Q @ K_transpose<br>[T,d_head] @ [d_head,T] &rarr; [T_query,T_key]</div><p><strong>Worked pair:</strong> q=[0,1] and k=[1,1] have dot product 0×1 + 1×1 = 1. Do this for every query/key pair. Row i contains the scores of query token i against candidate key positions. Different projection weights allow a token to ask for information using one representation and offer information using another.</p><p><strong>Why it is needed:</strong> a token representation can incorporate relevant context from other positions. Scores are not yet probabilities, and the highest score is not itself the output vector. Scaling, masks, normalization, and the weighted sum still follow. The detailed Q/K/V calculator below lets you inspect individual matrix cells.</p>`
  },
  {
    title: "Scale and mask: stabilize scores and prevent future leakage", short: "Scaling / masks",
    body: `<p><strong>Scaling:</strong> divide each query–key dot product by sqrt(d_head). If independent query/key coordinates have roughly unit variance, a sum over d_head products has variance growing with d_head. Scaling moderates that growth so softmax is less likely to become excessively peaked merely because the head is wider. It does not remove quadratic attention computation.</p><div class="equation">Scaled logits S = QK_transpose / sqrt(d_head)<br>Masked logits Z = S + M<br>M[i,j] = 0 if j &le; i; otherwise -infinity (causal self-attention)</div><p><strong>Causal masking:</strong> while predicting the next token, a decoder position cannot use future target tokens. Adding negative infinity before softmax makes their exponentials zero. Padding masks exclude padded keys; they solve a different problem from causal masks. Encoder self-attention is usually bidirectional over valid source tokens. Cross-attention can read the entire valid source, not future target tokens.</p><p><strong>Common bug:</strong> zero is not a forbidden logit: exp(0)=1, so it still receives probability. Mask before softmax, not after computing the output. An all-masked row needs deliberate handling because softmax over only negative infinity has no defined normalized distribution. Boolean-mask conventions can differ by API.</p>`
  },
  {
    title: "Attention softmax: turn scores into a weighted value mixture", short: "Attention softmax",
    body: `<p><strong>What happens:</strong> apply softmax across the key axis separately for each query row and each head. It turns allowed logits into nonnegative weights summing to one. For numerical stability, subtract the row maximum before exponentiating; this leaves the mathematical distribution unchanged.</p><div class="equation">a_j = exp(z_j - max(z)) / sum_k exp(z_k - max(z))<br>z = [2,1,-infinity] &rarr; a &asymp; [0.731,0.269,0]<br>V = [[1,0],[0,2],[9,9]]<br>O = a @ V &asymp; [0.731,0.538]</div><p><strong>Why multiply by V?</strong> the weights decide how much content to take from each allowed token. They do not return a token ID. The masked value [9,9] contributes nothing. In general [T_query,T_key] @ [T_key,d_value] produces [T_query,d_value]. Every component of the result is a weighted sum over key positions.</p><p><strong>Do not confuse two softmax operations:</strong> attention softmax normalizes over context positions, while the final language-model softmax normalizes over vocabulary entries. Attention weights alone are not a complete explanation of a model's answer. During training, attention dropout can modify the weights after softmax; this toy mixture omits dropout.</p>`
  },
  {
    title: "Multi-head attention: combine several learned matching spaces", short: "Multi-head attention",
    body: `<p><strong>What happens:</strong> instead of using one large attention head, project into H learned head spaces and compute attention independently within each. In ordinary multi-head attention with equal dimensions, d_head=d_model/H. Each head sees the same sequence through different learned Q/K/V projections—not an exclusive subset of tokens.</p><div class="equation">Q/K/V: [B,T,d_model]<br>reshape &rarr; [B,T,H,d_head]<br>transpose &rarr; [B,H,T,d_head]<br>head_h = softmax(Q_h K_h_transpose / sqrt(d_head) + M) V_h<br>Output = Concat(head_1,...,head_H) W_O</div><p><strong>Example:</strong> d_model=8 and H=2 give two heads of width 4. Each head has its own [T,T] attention matrix. Concatenation restores width 8; W_O mixes the concatenated features and returns the sublayer output to the model width required by the residual addition.</p><p><strong>Why it helps:</strong> separate matching spaces can represent different relationships simultaneously. Do not assign guaranteed roles such as "head 1 always handles grammar": specialization is learned and must be investigated, not assumed. GQA/MQA share fewer K/V heads across query heads; the equal-Q/K/V-head diagram describes ordinary MHA.</p>`
  },
  {
    title: "Residual connection: add an update, do not replace the state", short: "Residual addition",
    body: `<p><strong>What happens:</strong> add a sublayer's output to its input residual stream, element by element. Attention provides an update to existing token features rather than being the only route by which those features reach the next layer. An analogous residual surrounds the feed-forward sublayer.</p><div class="equation">y = x + F(x)<br>x = [1,2], F(x) = [0.1,-0.2] &rarr; y = [1.1,1.8]<br>Both branches must have compatible shapes [B,T,d_model]</div><p><strong>Why it is needed:</strong> residuals provide a direct additive information path and an identity term in the local derivative, d y / d x = I + J_F(x). This helps optimize deep networks, but does not guarantee that gradients can never vanish or explode. Sublayers can learn refinements instead of having to reconstruct the entire incoming representation.</p><p><strong>On the diagram:</strong> follow the curved skip arrow around attention or the MLP to the addition point. It is not concatenation, multiplication, an average, or normalization. In a pre-norm attention sublayer the actual update is x + Attention(Norm(x)); in the original post-norm layout it is Norm(x + Attention(x)). Those are different computations.</p>`
  },
  {
    title: "LayerNorm and RMSNorm: normalize features at each token", short: "LayerNorm / RMSNorm",
    body: `<p><strong>LayerNorm:</strong> for each token independently, compute the mean and variance across its hidden-feature axis. Center, rescale, then apply learned scale gamma and offset beta. This does not average across sequence positions or combine examples in the batch.</p><div class="equation">LN(x) = gamma * (x - mean(x)) / sqrt(var(x) + epsilon) + beta<br>x=[1,3], mean=2, variance=1<br>With gamma=1, beta=0 and ignoring epsilon: LN(x)=[-1,1]</div><p><strong>Why it is needed:</strong> controlling feature scales helps training behave reliably. Epsilon prevents division by zero for constant vectors. Learned affine parameters let the model adapt the normalized features. RMSNorm instead divides by a root-mean-square statistic without the same mean-centering step; it is not interchangeable with LayerNorm by definition.</p><p><strong>Read the diagram's layout carefully:</strong> the original encoder–decoder uses post-norm "Add &amp; Norm." The illustrated modern decoder-only variant uses pre-norm: normalize before each sublayer and keep the residual addition outside it, followed by a final normalization after the stack. Architecture variants differ, so name which layout you are explaining.</p>`
  },
  {
    title: "Feed-forward network: nonlinear feature processing per token", short: "Feed-forward / MLP",
    body: `<p><strong>What happens:</strong> apply the same small neural network to every token position independently within a layer. The original form is FFN(x)=ReLU(xW_1+b_1)W_2+b_2, expanding from d_model to d_ff and projecting back. Its weights are shared across positions, but normally differ between transformer layers.</p><div class="equation">[B,T,d_model] &rarr; [B,T,d_ff] &rarr; nonlinearity &rarr; [B,T,d_model]<br>Toy: [-1,2] &rarr; [-1,2,-1,-2] &rarr; ReLU [0,2,0,0] &rarr; [0,2]</div><p><strong>Why it is needed:</strong> attention gathers information across positions; the FFN transforms features at each position. Nonlinearity allows more than a single linear map. Without a nonlinearity between two linear projections, their composition collapses to another linear projection. The output returns to d_model for the residual sum.</p><p><strong>Variants:</strong> modern models may use GELU, SiLU, or gated forms such as SwiGLU, where one projected feature branch gates another before the output projection. Do not assume every MLP uses ReLU or a fixed expansion factor of four. The toy values illustrate computation only; they are not learned features from a real checkpoint.</p>`
  },
  {
    title: "Cross-attention: let the decoder read encoder memory", short: "Cross-attention",
    body: `<p><strong>What changes from self-attention:</strong> a sequence-to-sequence decoder forms Q from its current target-side hidden states, while K and V are projections of the encoder outputs. The encoder has already represented the source. Cross-attention lets each target position retrieve useful source information while generating the target sequence.</p><div class="equation">Q: [B,H,T_target,d_head]<br>K,V: [B,H,S_source,d_head]<br>Scores: [B,H,T_target,S_source]<br>For a 3-token target and 5-token source, one head has a 3&times;5 score matrix</div><p><strong>Example:</strong> when translating, a target word can attend to the relevant source word or phrase even when their positions differ. Source padding is masked. There is generally no target-style triangular mask over the source axis: the full valid source is available.</p><p><strong>On the diagram:</strong> follow the encoder-to-decoder side arrow marked K,V into the decoder's second attention sublayer. Its Q arrives from the decoder's preceding sublayer. The illustrated encoder-only and decoder-only families have no such cross-attention block. Putting retrieved text into a decoder-only prompt creates self-attention over that prompt; it does not automatically add an encoder–decoder cross-attention module.</p>`
  },
  {
    title: "Repeat blocks: build progressively contextual hidden states", short: "Repeated blocks",
    body: `<p><strong>What happens:</strong> pass the sequence through L transformer blocks. Each block keeps the outer hidden shape [B,T,d_model] while updating its contents through attention and feed-forward operations. In a normal dense stack, different layers have different parameters. The "&times; L" label means repeated architecture, not repeatedly calling one shared set of weights unless the model explicitly ties them.</p><div class="equation">X^(0) = token representations + applicable positional handling<br>X^(l+1) = Block_l(X^(l)), for l=0,...,L-1<br>Shape remains [B,T,d_model]; representations change</div><p><strong>Why depth matters:</strong> later layers can compose features derived by earlier layers. There is no universal rule that a particular numbered layer always represents syntax or reasoning. Layer roles depend on the trained model and task; measure them rather than memorizing a story.</p><p><strong>Cost:</strong> more layers increase parameters, compute, activations during training, and KV state during cached generation. Dense self-attention has quadratic sequence-length arithmetic, while the per-layer KV cache grows linearly with processed sequence length. Repeating blocks does not repeatedly tokenize the text or produce one final token per layer.</p>`
  },
  {
    title: "Vocabulary projection: hidden features become token logits", short: "Vocabulary projection",
    body: `<p><strong>What happens:</strong> after the final stack output (and final normalization when the architecture uses one), a learned linear head maps each hidden vector to one real-valued score per vocabulary token. These scores are logits, not probabilities and not token IDs.</p><div class="equation">Z = H_final W_vocab + b<br>[B,T,d_model] @ [d_model,V_vocab] &rarr; [B,T,V_vocab]<br>One toy position: logits for [cat,dog,EOS] = [2,1,0]</div><p><strong>Why it is needed:</strong> the hidden width is a feature-space dimension; the vocabulary is a set of candidate symbols. The output head connects them. Some models tie this head's weights to the input embedding table; weight tying is an architecture choice, not required for every transformer.</p><p><strong>Which position predicts what?</strong> a causal hidden state at position t sees tokens through t and produces scores for the token at t+1. During generation, the final prompt position provides the first new-token logits. Earlier positions' logits are also available, but are not separate next-token choices to append to the current end of the sequence.</p>`
  },
  {
    title: "Vocabulary softmax: probabilities over possible next tokens", short: "Output softmax",
    body: `<p><strong>What happens:</strong> normalize the output logits across the vocabulary axis. For logits [2,1,0], subtract 2, exponentiate [0,-1,-2], and divide by their sum. The result is approximately [0.665,0.245,0.090]. It is a distribution for the next symbol, not proof that the most likely statement is factually correct.</p><div class="equation">p(token=j | prefix) = exp(z_j - max(z)) / sum_k exp(z_k - max(z))<br>[cat,dog,EOS] &rarr; [0.665,0.245,0.090]</div><p><strong>Different from attention:</strong> the attention distribution chooses proportions of contextual value vectors; this distribution chooses among vocabulary candidates. The two axes can coincidentally have the same size in a toy example, but their semantics are different.</p><p><strong>Decoding:</strong> greedy selection takes the maximum-logit token; sampling draws from a distribution, potentially after temperature or top-k/top-p processing. For temperature tau&gt;0, divide logits by tau before softmax. Lower temperature sharpens the distribution. Practical "temperature 0" usually requests greedy behavior rather than literally dividing by zero. During training, stable cross-entropy implementations consume logits directly; do not apply an extra softmax before a logits-based loss.</p>`
  },
  {
    title: "Autoregressive generation: append one token and run again", short: "Generation loop",
    body: `<p><strong>What happens:</strong> process the prompt, obtain final-position logits, select one next token, append it, then repeat. Stop on the configured end-of-sequence token, output-length limit, or another explicit stopping rule. Generating one token does not mean running only one transformer layer: each generated token depends on the full relevant stack computation.</p><div class="equation">Prompt &rarr; logits &rarr; select "cat"<br>Prompt + "cat" &rarr; logits &rarr; select next token<br>Repeat until a stopping condition</div><p><strong>KV caching:</strong> retain previously computed keys and values at each layer so the next step need not re-project the entire prefix. The new query still attends to relevant cached entries. A newly sampled token joins the cache only when it is fed back and processed. Prefill handles the prompt; decode processes successive feedback tokens.</p><p><strong>Encoder–decoder distinction:</strong> encode the source once for a fixed input, then reuse encoder memory while generating target tokens. Decoder self-attention state grows with target length; projected cross-attention K/V may also be cached. A decoder-only model has no separate encoder pass or encoder-memory cross-attention in this illustrated architecture.</p>`
  },
  {
    title: "Teacher forcing: train parallel positions without seeing the future", short: "Training vs inference",
    body: `<p><strong>Training example:</strong> for tokens [BOS,I,like,ML,EOS], use input [BOS,I,like,ML] and labels [I,like,ML,EOS]. A causal mask lets all positions be evaluated in parallel while ensuring each prediction sees only its allowed prefix. Ground-truth earlier tokens are supplied as inputs: this is teacher forcing, not future-label leakage.</p><div class="equation">Input:  BOS | I    | like | ML<br>Label:  I   | like | ML   | EOS<br>Loss = mean negative log probability of valid target tokens</div><p><strong>Learning:</strong> cross-entropy compares predicted next-token logits with labels, backpropagation computes parameter gradients, and the optimizer updates embeddings, projections, normalization parameters, MLPs, and other trainable components. Mask padding or other excluded targets according to the training objective. Some model APIs shift labels internally; avoid shifting twice.</p><p><strong>Inference:</strong> there are no target labels or optimizer updates. The model conditions on its own selected tokens rather than the full ground-truth continuation. Earlier generation errors can change later context. The architecture diagram describes the forward computation; SFT, DPO, and PPO change training data/objectives and update rules, not the definition of a residual addition or softmax.</p>`
  }
];

const transformerLesson = GUIDE.lessons.find(lesson => lesson.id === "transformers");
transformerLesson.time = 75;
transformerLesson.sections = [
  transformerLesson.sections[0],
  ...TRANSFORMER_STEPS.map((step, index) => ({ title: `Step ${String(index + 1).padStart(2, "0")}: ${step.title}`, body: step.body, diagramStep: index })),
  ...transformerLesson.sections.filter((section, index) => ["Trace every tensor from input to output", "Position, context, and complexity", "Work through a tiny example"].includes(section.title) || index === transformerLesson.sections.length - 1)
];

GUIDE.lessons.splice(GUIDE.lessons.findIndex(lesson => lesson.id === "training") + 1, 0, {
  id: "gpt-training", stage: 0, title: "How a GPT-style model is trained",
  subtitle: "From raw data and random weights to an evaluated assistant",
  time: 65, tags: ["GPT", "pretraining", "AdamW", "distributed training", "post-training"], icon: "",
  objective: "Whiteboard a complete GPT-style training pipeline, derive its token-weighted loss, explain an optimizer update, and separate pretraining from assistant post-training.",
  sections: [
    { title: "The complete lifecycle: a model is more than one training run", body: `<p>GPT stands for <strong>Generative Pre-trained Transformer</strong>. A GPT-style base model is typically a causal, decoder-only transformer trained to predict the next token in large text corpora. Its weights are learned through repeated forward passes, loss computation, backpropagation, and optimizer updates. An assistant may then undergo instruction tuning and preference-based post-training, followed by independent evaluation and deployment engineering.</p><div class="viz-flow-row" aria-label="GPT-style training lifecycle"><div class="viz-node"><strong>Governed data</strong><small>Provenance, filtering, splits</small></div><span class="viz-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span><div class="viz-node"><strong>Tokenizer + batches</strong><small>IDs, targets, masks</small></div><span class="viz-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span><div class="viz-node accent"><strong>Causal pretraining</strong><small>Predict, compare, backpropagate</small></div><span class="viz-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span><div class="viz-node"><strong>Post-training</strong><small>Demonstrations / preferences</small></div><span class="viz-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span><div class="viz-node"><strong>Release gates</strong><small>Quality, safety, serving</small></div></div><p><strong>Scope:</strong> this is an established, public training pattern, not a reconstruction of any undisclosed commercial model. GPT-family releases differ. GPT-3 established large-scale autoregressive pretraining and few-shot prompting; InstructGPT describes an SFT, reward-model, and PPO pipeline. DPO is an alternative preference method, not a claim about every GPT release. The GPT-4 report does not disclose a complete architecture, dataset, compute, or hyperparameter recipe.</p>` },
    { title: "Step 1: define the target behavior and protect evaluation first", body: `<p>Decide what the model should do, which languages/domains it must support, its context length, licensing/privacy constraints, and training/serving budgets. Specify whether the goal is a base next-token model or an instruction-following assistant. These are different deliverables: completing plausible text is not the same as reliably following a request.</p><p>Create held-out evaluation sets before repeatedly tuning the pipeline. Separate training documents from related validation/test documents using duplicate grouping and appropriate time boundaries. Track source overlap, benchmark contamination, and downstream instruction/preference leakage. Test data must not quietly enter pretraining, SFT examples, reward-model data, or model-selection prompts.</p><p><strong>Artifact:</strong> a versioned requirements and evaluation contract listing datasets, slices, metrics, acceptance thresholds, and exclusions. Include loss/perplexity, task accuracy, factuality, instruction adherence, unsafe behavior/refusals, bias, privacy risks, and deployment performance where relevant. One aggregate benchmark score is not a complete release criterion.</p>` },
    { title: "Step 2: collect, filter, deduplicate, and version the corpus", body: `<p>Start from authorized sources with provenance and permitted uses. Parse documents, normalize encoding, filter corrupted/spam/low-quality material, and apply appropriate privacy and content policies. Deduplicate exact and near-duplicate content across sources and evaluation splits. Excessive duplication changes training exposure and can encourage memorization; removing every repeated phrase would also destroy useful structure, so validate the policy.</p><p>Choose and monitor a mixture of domains and languages. If a small domain is oversampled, its effective number of passes can be much larger than the global corpus average. Synthetic examples require provenance and quality checks too; generated text is not automatically correct or representative.</p><p><strong>Artifact:</strong> a reproducible data manifest: source/version, transformations, deduplication rules, mixture weights, token counts, exclusions, and dataset hashes. Count tokens actually consumed, not just bytes scraped. Inspect examples and source-level quality distributions rather than trusting a single filter score. Preserve the ability to investigate and remove problematic data in later versions.</p>` },
    { title: "Step 3: train or select a tokenizer and assemble sequences", body: `<p>A subword/byte-oriented tokenizer maps text to vocabulary IDs. Freeze and version its vocabulary, normalization, special tokens, and encoding rules before the associated model run. Evaluate compression, rare names, code, multilingual text, and pathological inputs. Changing tokenization changes sequence lengths and the prediction problem; perplexities across different tokenizers are not directly comparable.</p><p>Convert documents into training sequences up to the configured context length. Padding produces unused positions; packing can improve utilization by combining examples. Record document boundaries and use the intended EOS, attention, position, and loss policies. <strong>An EOS token does not itself enforce attention isolation.</strong> If packed examples must not see each other, construct a block-diagonal/segmented attention policy and verify it.</p><p>Do not confuse an attention mask with a loss mask. The attention mask controls which tokens can supply context; the loss mask controls which targets contribute supervision. A token excluded from loss may still be visible as context. Shuffling, sampling weights, document truncation, and packing decisions are part of the reproducible training recipe.</p>` },
    { title: "Step 4: configure the decoder and initialize parameters", body: `<p>Choose vocabulary size, model width, layer count, query/KV-head counts, feed-forward dimensions, normalization, positional handling, activation functions, dropout, and context limits. Ensure tensor shapes and residual widths match. A from-scratch run normally starts from initialized parameters; continued pretraining starts from an existing checkpoint and is a different experiment.</p><p>The model contains learned token embeddings, attention projections, MLPs, normalization parameters, and an output head; input/output weights may be tied. Initialization and residual scaling affect optimization stability. Do not copy a larger model's learning rate or initialization policy without testing the resulting scale.</p><p><strong>Before scaling:</strong> test one tiny synthetic batch. Verify shapes, finite logits/loss/gradients, causal invariance, padding behavior, registered parameters, gradient flow, and save/reload parity. Overfit that batch as a wiring check. It demonstrates that the loop can fit a fixture—not that the model generalizes or that the full dataset is clean. Use the transformer and PyTorch chapters to inspect the actual forward computation.</p>` },
    { title: "Step 5: create shifted next-token targets", body: `<p>For a sequence [BOS,I,like,ML,EOS], the position consuming BOS predicts I, the position consuming I predicts like, and so on. Causal masking permits all these positions to be processed in parallel without allowing a prediction to use future target tokens.</p><div class="viz-comparison-scroll"><table class="concept-table"><caption>Teacher-forced causal training</caption><thead><tr><th>Position</th><th>Input token</th><th>Target token</th><th>Visible input prefix</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0</td><td>BOS</td><td>I</td><td>BOS</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>I</td><td>like</td><td>BOS, I</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>like</td><td>ML</td><td>BOS, I, like</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>ML</td><td>EOS</td><td>BOS, I, like, ML</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The forward pass yields logits [B,T,V_vocab]. Align each supervised position with exactly one next-token target. Some Hugging Face causal-LM APIs shift labels internally; a manual cross-entropy implementation usually aligns shifted slices explicitly. Check the specific API and do not shift twice. Ignore padded or deliberately excluded targets, commonly with ignore_index=-100. This ignore value is a loss convention, not a vocabulary ID to feed into embedding lookup.</p>` },
    { title: "Step 6: compute a stable, token-weighted pretraining loss", body: `<div class="equation">L = - sum_valid log p_theta(target | allowed prefix) / N_valid<br>Example target probabilities: [0.5, 0.25, 0.125]<br>Token losses: [0.6931, 1.3863, 2.0794]<br>Mean loss = 1.3863 nats; perplexity = exp(loss) = 4</div><p>Compute cross-entropy from logits using a stable log-softmax operation. Do not softmax the logits first and then pass probabilities into a loss expecting logits. Count only valid supervised tokens in the denominator; padded targets should not dilute the loss. Fail visibly or deliberately skip an all-masked batch under a documented policy instead of dividing by zero and reporting a plausible loss.</p><p>The objective rewards probability on the observed next token, not factual verification. A model can learn fluent mistakes, stereotypes, or memorized sequences from its corpus. Validation loss is useful, but it does not replace task, grounding, safety, or privacy evaluation. Report both the tokenization/corpus definition and the aggregation method when comparing perplexity.</p>` },
    { title: "Step 7: backpropagate and update weights with AdamW", body: `<div class="viz-flow-row" aria-label="One optimizer update"><div class="viz-node"><strong>Forward</strong><small>Parameters produce logits</small></div><span class="viz-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span><div class="viz-node"><strong>Loss</strong><small>Compare with shifted targets</small></div><span class="viz-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span><div class="viz-node accent"><strong>Backward</strong><small>Compute parameter gradients</small></div><span class="viz-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span><div class="viz-node"><strong>Optimizer step</strong><small>Update parameters and state</small></div><span class="viz-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span><div class="viz-node"><strong>Repeat</strong><small>Next data window</small></div></div><p>Autograd applies the chain rule through the output head, blocks, and embeddings to compute gradients. Gradients indicate local changes to the chosen loss; they are not labels saying a feature is true. AdamW maintains moving first and second gradient moments, applies bias correction, and uses an adaptive update with decoupled weight decay. Parameter groups may exclude biases/normalization parameters from decay according to the recipe.</p><p>A learning-rate schedule commonly includes warmup followed by a decay policy, but exact values are run-specific. Clip gradients when appropriate and log their norms. With loss scaling, unscale gradients before clipping. Step the optimizer once per intended effective batch, advance the scheduler according to its defined unit, and clear gradients at the correct boundary. Sampling the next token is not how gradients are computed: ordinary pretraining differentiates the supervised loss through logits.</p>` },
    { title: "Step 8: batch correctly and accumulate gradients without bias", body: `<p>A microbatch is the data processed by one forward/backward pass. Accumulation collects gradients across several microbatches before an optimizer update. With ordinary data parallelism, an effective sequence batch is approximately microbatch size per device × accumulation steps × data-parallel workers. Token counts are often more informative because sequence lengths and target masks vary.</p><div class="equation">Correct window loss = sum_i N_i L_i / sum_i N_i<br>Microbatch A: N=2, mean loss=0.5<br>Microbatch B: N=6, mean loss=2.0<br>Token-weighted loss = (2*0.5 + 6*2.0)/8 = 1.625<br>Unweighted mean of means = 1.25 (different objective)</div><p>Dividing every microbatch mean by the accumulation count gives the intended token mean only when the relevant token weights are equal. A robust conceptual approach is to sum valid-token losses and normalize by the intended window denominator. Distributed training must additionally account for gradient averaging and global valid-token counts. Test the accumulated update against an equivalent combined batch on a deterministic tiny fixture. Accumulation does not shrink the activations of an individual microbatch or automatically match every large-batch training dynamic.</p>` },
    { title: "Step 9: use mixed precision and distributed memory deliberately", body: `<p>Training memory includes model weights, gradients, optimizer moments, activations, temporary buffers, and communication workspaces. Inference KV-cache formulas do not describe the full training footprint. BF16/FP16 can reduce memory and improve supported hardware throughput, while sensitive reductions/optimizer state may use higher precision. FP16 commonly needs loss scaling; BF16 generally has a wider exponent range, but still requires numerical checks.</p><p>Data parallelism processes different examples on replicated models and synchronizes gradients. Sharded approaches such as FSDP/ZeRO distribute selected parameters, gradients, or optimizer state. Tensor parallelism splits operations; pipeline parallelism splits layers and schedules microbatches. These are different axes with different communication and utilization costs.</p><p>Activation checkpointing trades recomputation for saved activation memory. FlashAttention reduces attention IO/materialization; it is not the same as sharding optimizer state and does not make dense attention arithmetic linear in context length. Profile actual step time, valid tokens/second, device utilization, communication stalls, and failures. More GPUs do not guarantee proportional speedup. See the CUDA chapter for timing asynchronous work correctly.</p>` },
    { title: "Step 10: balance model size, data tokens, and compute", body: `<p>More parameters are not always the best use of a fixed budget. The amount and quality of training data, optimization schedule, context length, hardware efficiency, and downstream serving cost also matter. Compute-optimal scaling studies motivate balancing model size and consumed training tokens; a particular tokens-per-parameter ratio is not a universal law.</p><div class="equation">Rough dense-transformer training estimate: FLOPs &asymp; 6 * P * D<br>P = parameter count; D = consumed training tokens<br>P=100 million, D=1 billion &rarr; roughly 6&times;10^17 FLOPs</div><p>This is a planning approximation, not an exact measurement: accounting conventions, attention at long context, recomputation, architectural sparsity, embeddings, and hardware utilization change the result. Convert a budget into an experiment plan using measured throughput and reserve time for evaluation, checkpointing, interruptions, and failed runs. Consumed tokens can exceed unique corpus tokens when documents are repeated. Track both. Compare smaller/longer-trained and larger/shorter-trained candidates using held-out quality and realistic inference cost, not parameter count alone.</p>` },
    { title: "Step 11: validate throughout training and diagnose failures", body: `<p>Track training and held-out token loss, learning rate, gradient norms, update norms, valid tokens, throughput, memory, and data-source mix. Evaluate selected task suites at controlled intervals with versioned prompts and decoding. Report uncertainty and slices rather than selecting a model from one noisy headline score.</p><p>If loss becomes nonfinite, inspect targets, masks, dtype behavior, learning rate, gradients, and corrupted batches. If training improves while validation worsens, investigate overfitting, distribution mismatch, and data leakage. A sudden loss spike has multiple possible causes; inspect evidence rather than immediately increasing clipping or skipping every difficult batch.</p><p>Evaluate instruction following, factuality, abstention, sensitive-data memorization risks, bias, and adversarial behavior independently as appropriate. Hold evaluation setup fixed when attributing improvements. A base model's validation perplexity can improve while assistant usefulness or a particular safety slice regresses. Keep final tests separate from the repeated model-selection loop.</p>` },
    { title: "Step 12: checkpoint for actual resumption, not just inference", body: `<p>An inference export may contain model weights and configuration, but a faithful training restart needs more: optimizer moments, learning-rate scheduler state, update/token counters, RNG state, data sampler/shuffle state, stream cursor, tokenizer/config versions, and the data manifest. Include mixed-precision scaler and sharding metadata when relevant.</p><p>Write checkpoints with integrity checks and a documented completion boundary; avoid advertising a half-written checkpoint as restorable. Test interrupted-run recovery on a small job. Confirm whether the data pipeline repeats or drops samples across an interruption. Merely loading identical weights does not restore AdamW's next update or the position in the dataset.</p><p>Exact reproducibility may depend on hardware, kernels, distributed topology, and nondeterministic operations. State the reproducibility level you verified. Preserve operational rollback checkpoints separately from the "best" evaluation candidate and retain the evidence needed to understand why a model was selected.</p>` },
    { title: "Step 13: instruction-tune the base model with demonstrations", body: `<p>A pretrained base model learns text continuation. SFT supplies examples of desired responses to instructions, conversation history, evidence, and tool schemas. Match the tokenizer/chat template used at inference and supervise the intended target spans—often assistant responses rather than user/system context. Keep evaluation and duplicated conversations out of the training split.</p><p>The usual objective remains token-level cross-entropy; the input distribution and supervised behavior change. Full fine-tuning updates many weights. LoRA/QLoRA adapt selected weights more economically, with their own precision, optimizer, and serving considerations. SFT is not a reliable replacement for retrieving frequently changing facts.</p><p>Include examples of clarification, uncertainty, appropriate refusal, grounded answers, and valid tool-call formats where required. Compare base versus SFT with the same evidence, prompts, and decoding so a retrieval improvement is not misattributed to weight updates. Continue with the <a href="#lesson/sft">SFT chapter</a> and the <a href="#coding">PyTorch shifted-loss exercise</a>.</p>` },
    { title: "Step 14: learn preferences with a method-specific objective", body: `<p>In the InstructGPT-style RLHF pipeline, annotators compare outputs for the same prompt; a reward model learns to rank preferred responses. PPO then updates an SFT-initialized policy from sampled rollouts using reward/advantage estimates and controls such as reference-policy KL regularization. The reward model is a proxy, so reward hacking and regressions require independent evaluation.</p><p>DPO instead trains directly on chosen/rejected response pairs using policy-versus-reference log probabilities under its objective. Standard DPO does not require the separate reward-model training and online PPO rollout loop of that pipeline. It is an alternative branch, not necessarily a mandatory stage after PPO. Other post-training methods exist.</p><p>Preference wins are not guarantees of correctness, calibrated uncertainty, privacy, or safety. Audit annotation consistency, length/style bias, prompt coverage, and reward exploitation. Keep a held-out evaluation rubric distinct from the optimized preference signal. See the <a href="#lesson/dpo">DPO</a> and <a href="#lesson/ppo">PPO</a> chapters for derivations; do not claim a particular proprietary release uses one without a public source.</p>` },
    { title: "Step 15: release an evaluated model, then monitor the system", body: `<p>Select a checkpoint using agreed release gates, not just the lowest training loss. Version model weights/adapters, tokenizer, chat template, context settings, generation policy, and any safety or tool-execution controls. Validate quantization/export/kernel changes against the reference model before measuring serving gains.</p><p>Measure task quality, factuality, behavior, memory, time to first token, inter-token latency, throughput, and cost under representative prompts and load. RAG, tool authorization, caches, routing, and UI behavior are additional system components; they are not all learned inside GPT weights. A better deployment can use the same trained model with better evidence and controls.</p><p>Roll out gradually with rollback and monitoring. Review distribution shifts, stale knowledge, user failure reports, and quality/cost regressions before collecting a new governed training set. Inference uses forward computation with appropriate evaluation/no-gradient settings; it does not automatically backpropagate from every user conversation. Learning from production interactions requires a separate authorized data and training process.</p>` },
    { title: "Whiteboard drill: defend one coherent training plan", body: `<p><strong>Scenario:</strong> build a synthetic-domain GPT-style model or adapt an existing authorized base model under a fixed budget. Explain why training from scratch or continued pretraining is justified instead of SFT or RAG alone. Specify the corpus mixture, protected evaluation, tokenizer, context/packing policy, architecture/checkpoint, token budget, optimizer, schedule, precision, and distributed strategy.</p><p>Draw one batch through IDs → embeddings → causal decoder → logits → shifted masked loss → backward → optimizer. Derive the valid-token denominator across unequal microbatches, then explain checkpoint resumption. Finally branch into SFT and a justified preference method, define independent quality/safety/serving gates, and name a rollback condition.</p><div class="callout"><strong>Interview trap</strong><p>"GPT is trained using reinforcement learning" is incomplete. Autoregressive pretraining is the foundation; demonstration/preference stages are distinct, method-dependent post-training processes. Neither a low next-token loss nor a high reward score alone establishes a trustworthy assistant.</p></div>` }
  ],
  sources: [
    ["GPT: Improving Language Understanding by Generative Pre-Training", "https://cdn.openai.com/research-covers/language-unsupervised/language_understanding_paper.pdf"],
    ["GPT-3: Language Models are Few-Shot Learners", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.14165"],
    ["InstructGPT: Training language models to follow instructions with human feedback", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.02155"],
    ["Training Compute-Optimal Large Language Models", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.15556"],
    ["GPT-4 Technical Report (public scope and limitations)", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.08774"]
  ],
  questions: [
    ["Two accumulated microbatches have different valid-target counts. Which loss preserves a token-mean objective?", ["Average their mean losses without weights", "Sum valid-token losses and divide by the intended total valid-token count, accounting for distributed reduction", "Divide by padded sequence positions", "Use only the final microbatch loss"], 1, "Unequal valid-token counts require token weighting. Distributed gradient averaging must also be included in the normalization contract."],
    ["Which describes a publicly established GPT-style training lifecycle?", ["All GPT models learn solely through PPO", "SFT eliminates the need for pretraining data", "Autoregressive pretraining learns next-token prediction; demonstrations and method-specific preference optimization can provide post-training", "DPO always requires first training a separate reward model"], 2, "Pretraining, SFT, and preference optimization have different data/objectives. InstructGPT describes an SFT/reward-model/PPO pipeline; standard DPO is an alternative, not a universal GPT recipe."],
    ["Why are model weights alone insufficient for a faithful AdamW training restart?", ["They do not restore optimizer moments, scheduler/RNG state, and the data position", "They cannot be used for inference", "They remove the causal mask permanently", "They make tokenization unnecessary"], 0, "Restoring weights does not restore the next optimizer update or which examples come next. Restart checkpoints need training and data-pipeline state in addition to the model."]
  ]
});

GUIDE.lessons.forEach((lesson, index) => {
  lesson.icon = String(index + 1).padStart(2, "0");
});
